{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788373519460,"sessionID":"ses_f9ca2427bffeVwNvwc6e496rx3","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8ae5cb4434f08c46","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-go/src/emit_go.rs"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-go/src/emit_go.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: //! The Go backend.\n2: //!\n3: //! Go is the first target that is both statically typed and has no runtime type information to\n4: //! fall back on, and that changes what an emitter has to do. Lua, JavaScript and jq all accept a\n5: //! depth-polymorphic helper -- one `tl_field(v, k, depth)` serves every shape -- because the\n6: //! value carries its own type. LLVM sidesteps the same problem by erasing everything into\n7: //! `tl_vec`. Go will not do either: a `[]User` and a `[][]User` are different types, so the\n8: //! distribution over dimensions has to be spelled out at each use site, with the intermediate\n9: //! types written in. The checker computed those types already, so what looks like extra work is\n10: //! really the first target that asks for what was always there.\n11: //!\n12: //! The second thing Go asks for is a *name*. A record type is structural here and anonymous\n13: //! everywhere else; Go needs a declared struct before a value of one can exist, so every record\n14: //! the program mentions is collected up front and given one.\n15: \n16: use std::collections::BTreeSet;\n17: \n18: use crate::ast::{BinOp, LogicOp};\n19: use crate::tir::{self, Builtin, Kind, LocalId, Program, Tir};\n20: use crate::ty::{self, Enums, Type};\n21: \n22: /// The binding the input value is read into. Unspellable in source, since every source name is\n23: /// prefixed.\n24: const INPUT: &str = \"t_input\";\n25: const INPUTS: &str = \"t_inputs\";\n26: \n27: /// Absence is a field rather than a nil pointer: `Opt<Vec<T>>` would otherwise have two\n28: /// spellings of empty, and a nil slice is a perfectly ordinary present value here.\n29: const OPT_TYPE: &str = r#\"type tlOpt[T any] struct {\n30: \tok bool\n31: \tv  T\n32: }\n33: \"#;\n34: \n35: const FAIL_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlFail(msg string) {\n36: \tfmt.Fprintln(os.Stderr, \"toylang: \"+msg)\n37: \tos.Exit(1)\n38: }\n39: \"#;\n40: \n41: const MAP_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlMap[A, B any](src []A, f func(A) B) []B {\n42: \tout := make([]B, len(src))\n43: \tfor i, e := range src {\n44: \t\tout[i] = f(e)\n45: \t}\n46: \treturn out\n47: }\n48: \"#;\n49: \n50: const SELECT_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlSelect[T any](src []T, pred func(T) bool) []T {\n51: \tout := []T{}\n52: \tfor _, e := range src {\n53: \t\tif pred(e) {\n54: \t\t\tout = append(out, e)\n55: \t\t}\n56: \t}\n57: \treturn out\n58: }\n59: \"#;\n60: \n61: const AT_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlAt[T any](v []T, i int32) tlOpt[T] {\n62: \tn := int32(len(v))\n63: \tif i < 0 {\n64: \t\ti = n + i\n65: \t}\n66: \tif i < 0 || i >= n {\n67: \t\treturn tlOpt[T]{}\n68: \t}\n69: \treturn tlOpt[T]{true, v[i]}\n70: }\n71: \"#;\n72: \n73: // Go's own slicing panics out of range, so the jq clamp has to be explicit: negatives count\n74: // from the end, then both bounds clamp to [0, n], and a crossed window is empty. A bound left\n75: // out is passed as its sentinel (`MinInt32` for the start, `MaxInt32` for the end), each of\n76: // which the clamp folds to the array's own boundary.\n77: const SLICE_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlSlice[T any](v []T, lo int32, hi int32) []T {\n78: \tn := int32(len(v))\n79: \tif lo < 0 {\n80: \t\tlo += n\n81: \t}\n82: \tif hi < 0 {\n83: \t\thi += n\n84: \t}\n85: \tif lo < 0 {\n86: \t\tlo = 0\n87: \t}\n88: \tif lo > n {\n89: \t\tlo = n\n90: \t}\n91: \tif hi < 0 {\n92: \t\thi = 0\n93: \t}\n94: \tif hi > n {\n95: \t\thi = n\n96: \t}\n97: \tif lo >= hi {\n98: \t\treturn []T{}\n99: \t}\n100: \treturn v[lo:hi]\n101: }\n102: \"#;\n103: \n104: const UNWRAP_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlUnwrap[T any](o tlOpt[T]) T {\n105: \tif !o.ok {\n106: \t\ttlFail(\"unwrapped a value that is not there\")\n107: \t}\n108: \treturn o.v\n109: }\n110: \"#;\n111: \n112: const TAIL_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlTail[T any](v []T) tlOpt[[]T] {\n113: \tif len(v) == 0 {\n114: \t\treturn tlOpt[[]T]{}\n115: \t}\n116: \treturn tlOpt[[]T]{true, v[1:]}\n117: }\n118: \"#;\n119: \n120: const FLATTEN_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlFlatten[T any](vv [][]T) []T {\n121: \tout := []T{}\n122: \tfor _, v := range vv {\n123: \t\tout = append(out, v...)\n124: \t}\n125: \treturn out\n126: }\n127: \"#;\n128: \n129: // `cmp.Ordered` is exactly the constraint the checker's own `orderable` restricts `sort`'s\n130: // element type to, so nothing here has to name Int, Int64, Str, or Char individually.\n131: const SORT_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlSort[T cmp.Ordered](v []T) []T {\n132: \tout := append([]T{}, v...)\n133: \tslices.Sort(out)\n134: \treturn out\n135: }\n136: \"#;\n137: \n138: const REVERSE_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlReverse[T any](v []T) []T {\n139: \tout := make([]T, len(v))\n140: \tfor i, x := range v {\n141: \t\tout[len(v)-1-i] = x\n142: \t}\n143: \treturn out\n144: }\n145: \"#;\n146: \n147: // `int32`/`int64` `+=` wraps by definition, so a fold needs nothing but the loop itself -- the\n148: // same \"the width wraps on its own\" story ARITH_HELPER and ARITH64_HELPER tell for one operator.\n149: const SUM_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlSum(v []int32) int32 {\n150: \tvar acc int32\n151: \tfor _, x := range v {\n152: \t\tacc += x\n153: \t}\n154: \treturn acc\n155: }\n156: \n157: func tlSum64(v []int64) int64 {\n158: \tvar acc int64\n159: \tfor _, x := range v {\n160: \t\tacc += x\n161: \t}\n162: \treturn acc\n163: }\n164: \"#;\n165: \n166: // `cmp.Ordered` covers both integer widths, so one helper serves Int and Int64. Absence is the\n167: // zero `tlOpt`, the same answer an out-of-range index gives.\n168: const MAX_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlMax[T cmp.Ordered](v []T) tlOpt[T] {\n169: \tif len(v) == 0 {\n170: \t\treturn tlOpt[T]{}\n171: \t}\n172: \tm := v[0]\n173: \tfor _, x := range v[1:] {\n174: \t\tif x > m {\n175: \t\t\tm = x\n176: \t\t}\n177: \t}\n178: \treturn tlOpt[T]{true, m}\n179: }\n180: \"#;\n181: \n182: /// Go's `int32` wraps on overflow by definition, and its `/` and `%` truncate toward zero, so\n183: /// only the zero divisor needs a guard. `MIN / -1` is defined to be `MIN` here, which is the\n184: /// wrapping answer the other backends were made to give.\n185: const ARITH_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlDiv(a, b int32) int32 {\n186: \tif b == 0 {\n187: \t\ttlFail(\"divided by zero\")\n188: \t}\n189: \treturn a / b\n190: }\n191: \n192: func tlRem(a, b int32) int32 {\n193: \tif b == 0 {\n194: \t\ttlFail(\"divided by zero\")\n195: \t}\n196: \treturn a % b\n197: }\n198: \"#;\n199: \n200: /// The same shape at 64 bits (kantord/toylang#83): Go defines `MIN / -1` as `MIN` for int64\n201: /// exactly as for int32, so only the zero divisor needs the guard here too.\n202: const ARITH64_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlDiv64(a, b int64) int64 {\n203: \tif b == 0 {\n204: \t\ttlFail(\"divided by zero\")\n205: \t}\n206: \treturn a / b\n207: }\n208: \n209: func tlRem64(a, b int64) int64 {\n210: \tif b == 0 {\n211: \t\ttlFail(\"divided by zero\")\n212: \t}\n213: \treturn a % b\n214: }\n215: \"#;\n216: \n217: // An enum value carries its payload behind a pointer (tlPtr below), so Go's own `==` on two\n218: // of them compares addresses: `circle{r: 1} == circle{r: 1}` was false here and true on\n219: // Python (kantord/toylang#68). reflect.DeepEqual is the structural walk, following the\n220: // pointer and comparing what it points at.\n221: const EQ_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlEq[T any](a, b T) bool { return reflect.DeepEqual(a, b) }\n222: \"#;\n223: \n224: /// Ad-hoc address-of: `&expr` only works on composite literals, and a payload is whatever\n225: /// expression the program wrote. The call inlines away.\n226: const PTR_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlPtr[T any](v T) *T { return &v }\n227: \"#;\n228: \n229: /// Go folds constant arithmetic exactly and rejects a result that does not fit, which is the\n230: /// direct opposite of the wrapping rule: `2147483647 + 1` is a compile error rather than\n231: /// `-2147483648`. Passing every literal through a function makes the expression non-constant, so\n232: /// the wrap happens at runtime where it is defined. The call inlines away.\n233: const INT_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlInt(n int32) int32 { return n }\n234: \"#;\n235: \n236: /// The same constant-folding escape at 64 bits: `tlInt64(9223372036854775807) + tlInt64(1)`\n237: /// must wrap at runtime rather than fail Go's exact constant arithmetic.\n238: const INT64_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlInt64(n int64) int64 { return n }\n239: \"#;\n240: \n241: // bufio.ScanLines strips a trailing \\r along with the \\n, which the other five backends do\n242: // not, so the split function is copied here with that one line removed rather than reused.\n243: const COLLECT_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlScanLines(data []byte, atEOF bool) (advance int, token []byte, err error) {\n244: \tif atEOF && len(data) == 0 {\n245: \t\treturn 0, nil, nil\n246: \t}\n247: \tif i := bytes.IndexByte(data, '\\n'); i >= 0 {\n248: \t\treturn i + 1, data[0:i], nil\n249: \t}\n250: \tif atEOF {\n251: \t\treturn len(data), data, nil\n252: \t}\n253: \treturn 0, nil, nil\n254: }\n255: \n256: func tlCollectLines() []string {\n257: \tout := []string{}\n258: \ts := bufio.NewScanner(os.Stdin)\n259: \ts.Buffer(make([]byte, 0, 65536), 1024*1024)\n260: \ts.Split(tlScanLines)\n261: \tfor s.Scan() {\n262: \t\tout = append(out, s.Text())\n263: \t}\n264: \treturn out\n265: }\n266: \"#;\n267: \n268: // RFC 4180 field splitting over the raw lines `tlCollectLines` keeps: a field wrapped in double\n269: // quotes may contain the delimiter, a doubled `\"\"` is a literal quote, and a quoted field may\n270: // span lines. The delimiter is matched literally, never as a pattern.\n271: const DSV_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlDsv(lines []string, sep string) [][]string {\n272: \tout := [][]string{}\n273: \trow := []string{}\n274: \tfield := \"\"\n275: \tinQuotes := false\n276: \tfor _, line := range lines {\n277: \t\ti := 0\n278: \t\tfor i < len(line) {\n279: \t\t\tif inQuotes {\n280: \t\t\t\tif line[i] == '\"' {\n281: \t\t\t\t\tif i+1 < len(line) && line[i+1] == '\"' {\n282: \t\t\t\t\t\tfield += \"\\\"\"\n283: \t\t\t\t\t\ti += 2\n284: \t\t\t\t\t} else {\n285: \t\t\t\t\t\tinQuotes = false\n286: \t\t\t\t\t\ti++\n287: \t\t\t\t\t}\n288: \t\t\t\t} else {\n289: \t\t\t\t\t_, size := utf8.DecodeRuneInString(line[i:])\n290: \t\t\t\t\tfield += line[i : i+size]\n291: \t\t\t\t\ti += size\n292: \t\t\t\t}\n293: \t\t\t} else if strings.HasPrefix(line[i:], sep) {\n294: \t\t\t\trow = append(row, field)\n295: \t\t\t\tfield = \"\"\n296: \t\t\t\ti += len(sep)\n297: \t\t\t} else if line[i] == '\"' && field == \"\" {\n298: \t\t\t\tinQuotes = true\n299: \t\t\t\ti++\n300: \t\t\t} else {\n301: \t\t\t\t_, size := utf8.DecodeRuneInString(line[i:])\n302: \t\t\t\tfield += line[i : i+size]\n303: \t\t\t\ti += size\n304: \t\t\t}\n305: \t\t}\n306: \t\tif inQuotes {\n307: \t\t\tfield += \"\\n\"\n308: \t\t} else {\n309: \t\t\trow = append(row, field)\n310: \t\t\tout = append(out, row)\n311: \t\t\trow = []string{}\n312: \t\t\tfield = \"\"\n313: \t\t}\n314: \t}\n315: \tif inQuotes {\n316: \t\tfield = field[:len(field)-1]\n317: \t\trow = append(row, field)\n318: \t\tout = append(out, row)\n319: \t}\n320: \treturn out\n321: }\n322: \"#;\n323: \n324: const RANGE_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlRange(n int32) []int32 {\n325: \tif n < 0 {\n326: \t\tn = 0\n327: \t}\n328: \tout := make([]int32, n)\n329: \tfor i := int32(0); i < n; i++ {\n330: \t\tout[i] = i\n331: \t}\n332: \treturn out\n333: }\n334: \"#;\n335: \n336: // Go's `for range` over a string already decodes UTF-8 into runes -- Unicode scalar values --\n337: // rather than bytes, so there is no decoding to get right here.\n338: const CHARS_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlChars(s string) []int32 {\n339: \tout := []int32{}\n340: \tfor _, r := range s {\n341: \t\tout = append(out, int32(r))\n342: \t}\n343: \treturn out\n344: }\n345: \"#;\n346: \n347: const JSONLINES_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlJsonlines[T any](v []T, f func(T) string) string {\n348: \tparts := make([]string, len(v))\n349: \tfor i, e := range v {\n350: \t\tparts[i] = f(e)\n351: \t}\n352: \treturn strings.Join(parts, \"\\n\")\n353: }\n354: \"#;\n355: \n356: const JOIN_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlJoin[T any](v []T, f func(T) string) string {\n357: \tparts := make([]string, len(v))\n358: \tfor i, e := range v {\n359: \t\tparts[i] = f(e)\n360: \t}\n361: \treturn \"[\" + strings.Join(parts, \",\") + \"]\"\n362: }\n363: \"#;\n364: \n365: /// Hand-written rather than `json.Marshal`, which escapes `<`, `>` and `&` and would print a\n366: /// different string than the other four backends for the same value.\n367: const QUOTE_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlQuote(s string) string {\n368: \tvar b strings.Builder\n369: \tb.WriteByte('\"')\n370: \tfor i := 0; i < len(s); i++ {\n371: \t\tc := s[i]\n372: \t\tswitch c {\n373: \t\tcase '\"':\n374: \t\t\tb.WriteString(\"\\\\\\\"\")\n375: \t\tcase '\\\\':\n376: \t\t\tb.WriteString(\"\\\\\\\\\")\n377: \t\tcase '\\n':\n378: \t\t\tb.WriteString(\"\\\\n\")\n379: \t\tcase '\\r':\n380: \t\t\tb.WriteString(\"\\\\r\")\n381: \t\tcase '\\t':\n382: \t\t\tb.WriteString(\"\\\\t\")\n383: \t\tdefault:\n384: \t\t\tif c < 0x20 {\n385: \t\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(&b, \"\\\\u%04x\", c)\n386: \t\t\t} else {\n387: \t\t\t\tb.WriteByte(c)\n388: \t\t\t}\n389: \t\t}\n390: \t}\n391: \tb.WriteByte('\"')\n392: \treturn b.String()\n393: }\n394: \"#;\n395: \n396: pub fn emit(program: &Program) -> String {\n397:     let mut used = Used::default();\n398:     let mut records = Vec::new();\n399:     let mut enums = Vec::new();\n400:     let mut ctx = Collect {\n401:         used: &mut used,\n402:         records: &mut records,\n403:         enums: &mut enums,\n404:         registry: &program.enums,\n405:     };\n406:     for f in &program.funcs {\n407:         if let Some(param_ty) = &f.param_ty {\n408:             ctx.ty(param_ty);\n409:         }\n410:         ctx.walk(&f.body);\n411:     }\n412:     ctx.walk(&program.body);\n413:     if let Some(ty) = &program.input {\n414:         ctx.ty(ty);\n415:     }\n416:     if let Some(ty) = &program.inputs {\n417:         ctx.ty(ty);\n418:     }\n419:     records.sort_by_key(|t| t.to_string());\n420:     enums.sort_by_key(|t| t.to_string());\n421: \n422:     let e = Emitter {\n423:         records,\n424:         enums,\n425:         registry: &program.enums,\n426:     };\n427: \n428:     let mut decls = String::new();\n429:     for (i, rec) in e.records.iter().enumerate() {\n430:         let Type::Record(fields) = rec else {\n431:             unreachable!(\"only records are collected\")\n432:         };\n433:         decls.push_str(&format!(\"type tlRec{i} struct {{\\n\"));\n434:         for (name, ty) in fields {\n435:             // Exported, because encoding/json only fills fields it can see. The `F` prefix keeps\n436:             // the mapping injective, so two toylang fields cannot collide on one Go field.\n437:             decls.push_str(&format!(\"\\tF{name} {} `json:\\\"{name}\\\"`\\n\", e.go_type(ty)));\n438:         }\n439:         decls.push_str(\"}\\n\\n\");\n440:     }\n441: \n442:     // Go has no sum type, so an enum is a tag (the variant's declaration index) plus one\n443:     // pointer per payload variant, nil except for the one the tag names.\n444:     for en in &e.enums {\n445:         let Type::Enum { name, .. } = en else {\n446:             unreachable!(\"only enums are collected\")\n447:         };\n448:         decls.push_str(&format!(\"type {} struct {{\\n\\ttag int32\\n\", e.go_type(en)));\n449:         for (i, (_, payload)) in ty::variants(&program.enums, en).iter().enumerate() {\n450:             if let Some(p) = payload {\n451:                 decls.push_str(&format!(\"\\tp{i} *{}\\n\", e.go_type(p)));\n452:             }\n453:         }\n454:         decls.push_str(\"}\\n\\n\");\n455:         // encoding/json cannot guess which of the enum's two wire shapes (ADR 0009) it is\n456:         // looking at, so decoding is spelled out. Only a program that reads input decodes.\n457:         if program.input.is_some() || program.inputs.is_some() {\n458:             decls.push_str(&e.enum_unmarshal(en, name));\n459:         }\n460:     }\n461: \n462:     decls.push_str(&e.printers(program));\n463: \n464:     // Package-level functions are visible in any order, so the forward reference the checker\n465:     // accepts needs nothing here. Lua wanted declarations and JavaScript relied on hoisting.\n466:     for f in &program.funcs {\n467:         let param = match (&f.param, &f.param_ty) {\n468:             (Some(name), Some(ty)) => format!(\"{} {}\", e.user(name), e.go_type(ty)),\n469:             (None, None) => String::new(),\n470:             _ => unreachable!(\"a function's param and param_ty agree\"),\n471:         };\n472:         decls.push_str(&format!(\n473:             \"func {}({}) {} {{\\n\\treturn {}\\n}}\\n\\n\",\n474:             e.user(&f.name),\n475:             param,\n476:             e.go_type(&f.body.ty),\n477:             e.expr(&f.body)\n478:         ));\n479:     }\n480: \n481:     if let Some(fusion) = tir::fusion(program) {\n482:         decls.push_str(&e.fused_main(program, &fusion));\n483:     } else {\n484:         decls.push_str(\"func main() {\\n\");\n485:         if let Some(ty) = &program.input {\n486:             decls.push_str(&format!(\"\\tvar {INPUT} {}\\n\", e.go_type(ty)));\n487:             decls.push_str(&format!(\n488:                 \"\\tif err := json.NewDecoder(os.Stdin).Decode(&{INPUT}); err != nil {{\\n\\t\\ttlFail(err.Error())\\n\\t}}\\n\"\n489:             ));\n490:         }\n491:         if let Some(ty) = &program.inputs {\n492:             // Decode in a loop until io.EOF rather than reading one array token: the wire format\n493:             // is consecutive top-level JSON values, one per line, not a single `[...]`.\n494:             decls.push_str(&format!(\n495:                 \"\\tvar {INPUTS} []{}\\n\\t{{\\n\\t\\tdec := json.NewDecoder(os.Stdin)\\n\\t\\tfor {{\\n\\t\\t\\tvar item {}\\n\\t\\t\\tif err := dec.Decode(&item); err != nil {{\\n\\t\\t\\t\\tif err == io.EOF {{\\n\\t\\t\\t\\t\\tbreak\\n\\t\\t\\t\\t}}\\n\\t\\t\\t\\ttlFail(err.Error())\\n\\t\\t\\t}}\\n\\t\\t\\t{INPUTS} = append({INPUTS}, item)\\n\\t\\t}}\\n\\t}}\\n\",\n496:                 e.go_type(ty),\n497:                 e.go_type(ty)\n498:             ));\n499:         }\n500:         let body = e.expr(&program.body);\n501:         // A top-level Str prints raw, the way jq's -r does; anything else prints as JSON.\n502:         let printed = if matches!(program.body.ty, Type::Str | Type::Sink) {\n503:             body\n504:         } else {\n505:             e.show(&program.body.ty, &body, 0)\n506:         };\n507:         decls.push_str(&format!(\"\\tfmt.Println({printed})\\n}}\\n\"));\n508:     }\n509: \n510:     // Which helpers to include is read off the emitted text rather than tracked alongside it. An\n511:     // unused function is not an error in Go, so a false positive costs nothing, while a missed\n512:     // one would not compile -- the asymmetry is what makes reading the output safe. Imports are\n513:     // the opposite (an unused one is an error), so those come from the walk instead.\n514:     let uses = |name: &str| decls.contains(name);\n515:     let unwrap = uses(\"tlUnwrap(\");\n516:     let arith = uses(\"tlDiv(\") || uses(\"tlRem(\");\n517:     let arith64 = uses(\"tlDiv64(\") || uses(\"tlRem64(\");\n518:     let collect = uses(\"tlCollectLines(\") || uses(\"tlScanLines\");\n519:     let fail = unwrap || arith || arith64 || program.input.is_some() || program.inputs.is_some();\n520:     let quote = uses(\"tlQuote(\");\n521:     let join = uses(\"tlJoin(\");\n522: \n523:     let mut helpers = String::new();\n524:     // tlOpt is what tlAt and tlUnwrap are written in terms of, and inference means the emitted\n525:     // text need never spell it. Helper-to-helper dependencies are stated rather than read back.\n526:     if uses(\"tlOpt[\") || uses(\"tlAt(\") || uses(\"tlTail(\") || unwrap {\n527:         helpers.push_str(OPT_TYPE);\n528:         helpers.push('\\n');\n529:     }\n530:     for (on, text) in [\n531:         (fail, FAIL_HELPER),\n532:         (uses(\"tlPtr(\"), PTR_HELPER),\n533:         (uses(\"tlEq(\"), EQ_HELPER),\n534:         (uses(\"tlInt(\"), INT_HELPER),\n535:         (uses(\"tlInt64(\"), INT64_HELPER),\n536:         (uses(\"tlMap(\"), MAP_HELPER),\n537:         (uses(\"tlSelect(\"), SELECT_HELPER),\n538:         (uses(\"tlAt(\"), AT_HELPER),\n539:         (uses(\"tlSlice(\"), SLICE_HELPER),\n540:         (uses(\"tlTail(\"), TAIL_HELPER),\n541:         (uses(\"tlFlatten(\"), FLATTEN_HELPER),\n542:         (uses(\"tlSort(\"), SORT_HELPER),\n543:         (uses(\"tlReverse(\"), REVERSE_HELPER),\n544:         (uses(\"tlSum(\") || uses(\"tlSum64(\"), SUM_HELPER),\n545:         (uses(\"tlMax(\"), MAX_HELPER),\n546:         (unwrap, UNWRAP_HELPER),\n547:         (arith, ARITH_HELPER),\n548:         (arith64, ARITH64_HELPER),\n549:         (uses(\"tlRange(\"), RANGE_HELPER),\n550:         (uses(\"tlChars(\"), CHARS_HELPER),\n551:         (collect, COLLECT_HELPER),\n552:         (uses(\"tlDsv(\"), DSV_HELPER),\n553:         (used.jsonlines, JSONLINES_HELPER),\n554:         (join, JOIN_HELPER),\n555:         (quote, QUOTE_HELPER),\n556:     ] {\n557:         if on {\n558:             helpers.push_str(text);\n559:             helpers.push('\\n');\n560:         }\n561:     }\n562: \n563:     // Go rejects an unused import, so each one is gated on the thing that spells it. Written as\n564:     // a table rather than a chain of `if`s, the same shape the helper text above already uses.\n565:     let reads_stdin = program.input.is_some() || program.inputs.is_some();\n566:     let mut imports = BTreeSet::new();\n567:     imports.insert(\"fmt\");\n568:     for (on, names) in [\n569:         (fail || reads_stdin || collect, &[\"os\"][..]),\n570:         (collect, &[\"bufio\", \"bytes\"]),\n571:         (reads_stdin, &[\"encoding/json\"]),\n572:         (program.inputs.is_some(), &[\"io\"]),\n573:         (join || quote || used.jsonlines || uses(\"tlDsv(\"), &[\"strings\"]),\n574:         (uses(\"tlDsv(\"), &[\"unicode/utf8\"]),\n575:         (uses(\"tlSort(\"), &[\"cmp\", \"slices\"]),\n576:         (uses(\"tlMax(\"), &[\"cmp\"]),\n577:         (uses(\"tlEq(\"), &[\"reflect\"]),\n578:         (\n579:             used.itoa\n580:                 || used.jsonlines_has_scalar\n581:                 || (program.body.ty != Type::Str && has_scalar(&program.enums, &program.body.ty)),\n582:             &[\"strconv\"],\n583:         ),\n584:     ] {\n585:         if on {\n586:             imports.extend(names);\n587:         }\n588:     }\n589: \n590:     let mut out = String::from(\"package main\\n\\nimport (\\n\");\n591:     for name in &imports {\n592:         out.push_str(&format!(\"\\t\\\"{name}\\\"\\n\"));\n593:     }\n594:     out.push_str(\")\\n\\n\");\n595:     out.push_str(&helpers);\n596:     out.push_str(&decls);\n597:     out\n598: }\n599: \n600: /// Whether printing this type reaches an Int or a Bool, which are the two `strconv` needs.\n601: fn has_scalar(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type) -> bool {\n602:     /// `seen` is what a recursive enum needs: its own payload leads back to it, and an enum\n603:     /// already under consideration answers nothing new.\n604:     fn reaches(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type, seen: &mut Vec<Type>) -> bool {\n605:         match ty {\n606:             // Only ever called on the program's own result type, which the checker guarantees is\n607:             // never a stream and never contains one.\n608:             Type::Stream(_) => unreachable!(\"a stream cannot reach has_scalar\"),\n609:             // The checker refuses a program whose result contains a Char, the same as a stream.\n610:             Type::Char => unreachable!(\"a Char cannot reach has_scalar\"),\n611:             Type::Int | Type::Int64 | Type::Bool => true,\n612:             // A Float prints as a plain number, which needs no strconv import.\n613:             Type::Float => false,\n614:             Type::Str => false,\n615:             Type::Sink => false,\n616:             Type::Vec(t) => reaches(enums, t, seen),\n617:             Type::Record(fields) => fields.iter().any(|(_, t)| reaches(enums, t, seen)),\n618:             Type::Enum { .. } => {\n619:                 if seen.contains(ty) {\n620:                     return false;\n621:                 }\n622:                 seen.push(ty.clone());\n623:                 ty::variants(enums, ty)\n624:                     .iter()\n625:                     .any(|(_, p)| p.as_ref().is_some_and(|p| reaches(enums, p, seen)))\n626:             }\n627:             Type::Param(_) => unreachable!(\"params are substituted before emit\"),\n628:         }\n629:     }\n630:     reaches(enums, ty, &mut Vec::new())\n631: }\n632: \n633: #[derive(Default)]\n634: struct Used {\n635:     itoa: bool,\n636:     /// Whether `jsonlines` was called at all, which decides whether `strings` needs importing.\n637:     jsonlines: bool,\n638:     /// Whether `strconv` is needed for a scalar inside a `jsonlines` element type, which the\n639:     /// ordinary `has_scalar` check on the program's own result type misses whenever that result\n640:     /// is exactly `Str` -- true for every `jsonlines` call, since that is what it returns.\n641:     jsonlines_has_scalar: bool,\n642: }\n643: \n644: /// One walk, collecting the record types that need declaring and the two builtins whose imports\n645: /// cannot be read back off the emitted text without risking a false positive.\n646: struct Collect<'a> {\n647:     used: &'a mut Used,\n648:     records: &'a mut Vec<Type>,\n649:     enums: &'a mut Vec<Type>,\n650:     /// Every enum the program declared. The variant list on a `Type::Enum` in hand may be a\n651:     /// placeholder, so the payloads to descend into are read from here (`ty::variants`).\n652:     registry: &'a Enums,\n653: }\n654: \n655: impl Collect<'_> {\n656:     fn ty(&mut self, t: &Type) {\n657:         match t {\n658:             Type::Vec(e) | Type::Stream(e) => self.ty(e),\n659:             Type::Record(fields) => {\n660:                 if !self.records.contains(t) {\n661:                     self.records.push(t.clone());\n662:                 }\n663:                 for (_, f) in fields {\n664:                     self.ty(f);\n665:                 }\n666:             }\n667:             // Opt keeps the tlOpt[T] struct it always had (already tagged), so it is not\n668:             // harvested as a declared enum; only what it holds is.\n669:             Type::Enum { .. } => {\n670:                 if let Some(inner) = t.as_opt() {\n671:                     self.ty(inner);\n672:                     return;\n673:                 }\n674:                 // Having it already is what stops a recursive enum here: its own payload leads\n675:                 // back to a type this list holds.\n676:                 if self.enums.contains(t) {\n677:                     return;\n678:                 }\n679:                 self.enums.push(t.clone());\n680:                 for (_, p) in ty::variants(self.registry, t) {\n681:                     if let Some(p) = p {\n682:                         self.ty(&p);\n683:                     }\n684:                 }\n685:             }\n686:             _ => {}\n687:         }\n688:     }\n689: \n690:     fn walk(&mut self, t: &Tir) {\n691:         self.ty(&t.ty);\n692:         match &t.kind {\n693:             Kind::Str(_)\n694:             | Kind::Int(_)\n695:             | Kind::Float(_)\n696:             | Kind::Var(_)\n697:             | Kind::Local(_)\n698:             | Kind::Input\n699:             | Kind::Inputs\n700:             | Kind::Lines\n701:             | Kind::Dsv { .. } => {}\n702:             Kind::VecLit(items) => items.iter().for_each(|i| self.walk(i)),\n703:             Kind::RecordLit { fields } => {\n704:                 fields.iter().for_each(|(_, v)| self.walk(v));\n705:             }\n706:             Kind::EnumLit { payload, .. } => {\n707:                 if let Some(p) = payload {\n708:                     self.walk(p);\n709:                 }\n710:             }\n711:             Kind::Call { arg, .. } => {\n712:                 if let Some(a) = arg {\n713:                     self.walk(a);\n714:                 }\n715:             }\n716:             Kind::Concat(l, r)\n717:             | Kind::Compare { lhs: l, rhs: r, .. }\n718:             | Kind::Logic { lhs: l, rhs: r, .. }\n719:             | Kind::Arith { lhs: l, rhs: r, .. } => {\n720:                 self.walk(l);\n721:                 self.walk(r);\n722:             }\n723:             Kind::Bind { value, body, .. } => {\n724:                 self.walk(value);\n725:                 self.walk(body);\n726:             }\n727:             Kind::Map { source, body, .. } | Kind::OptMap { source, body, .. } => {\n728:                 self.walk(source);\n729:                 self.walk(body);\n730:             }\n731:             Kind::Select { source, pred, .. } => {\n732:                 self.walk(source);\n733:                 self.walk(pred);\n734:             }\n735:             Kind::Field { base, .. } | Kind::Unwrap { base } | Kind::Not(base) => self.walk(base),\n736:             Kind::Index { base, index, .. } => {\n737:                 self.walk(base);\n738:                 self.walk(index);\n739:             }\n740:             Kind::Slice { base, start, end, .. } => {\n741:                 self.walk(base);\n742:                 if let Some(s) = start {\n743:                     self.walk(s);\n744:                 }\n745:                 if let Some(e) = end {\n746:                     self.walk(e);\n747:                 }\n748:             }\n749:             Kind::Match { subject, arms, .. } => {\n750:                 self.walk(subject);\n751:                 for a in arms {\n752:                     if let Some(g) = &a.guard {\n753:                         self.walk(g);\n754:                     }\n755:                     self.walk(&a.body);\n756:                 }\n757:             }\n758:             Kind::Builtin { which, arg } => {\n759:                 match which {\n760:                     Builtin::IntToStr => self.used.itoa = true,\n761:                     Builtin::JsonLines => {\n762:                         self.used.jsonlines = true;\n763:                         let elem =\n764:                             tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty).expect(\"checked to be a Vec or a stream\");\n765:                         self.used.jsonlines_has_scalar |= has_scalar(self.registry, elem);\n766:                     }\n767:                     // Purely textually gated below, like tlAt and tlRange: nothing here needs\n768:                     // the element type, so there is nothing to record on the walk.\n769:                     Builtin::IntToI64\n770:                     | Builtin::Range\n771:                     | Builtin::Collect\n772:                     | Builtin::Length\n773:                     | Builtin::Flatten\n774:                     | Builtin::Tail\n775:                     | Builtin::Fields\n776:                     | Builtin::Chars\n777:                     | Builtin::Sort\n778:                     | Builtin::Reverse\n779:                     | Builtin::Sum\n780:                     | Builtin::Max => {}\n781:                 }\n782:                 self.walk(arg);\n783:             }\n784:         }\n785:     }\n786: }\n787: \n788: struct Emitter<'a> {\n789:     records: Vec<Type>,\n790:     enums: Vec<Type>,\n791:     /// Every enum the program declared, for `ty::variants`: what a `Type::Enum` carries is a\n792:     /// placeholder wherever a recursive enum's payload reaches back to itself.\n793:     registry: &'a Enums,\n794: }\n795: \n796: impl Emitter<'_> {\n797:     fn user(&self, name: &str) -> String {\n798:         format!(\"v_{name}\")\n799:     }\n800: \n801:     fn local(&self, id: LocalId) -> String {\n802:         format!(\"t_{id}\")\n803:     }\n804: \n805:     fn go_type(&self, ty: &Type) -> String {\n806:         match ty {\n807:             Type::Str => \"string\".to_string(),\n808:             // A sink is a joined string at runtime, so a `-> Sink` function has one here too.\n809:             Type::Sink => \"string\".to_string(),\n810:             // The default Int is 32 bits and wraps, and Go's int32 does exactly that for free.\n811:             Type::Int => \"int32\".to_string(),\n812:             // Same story a word wider (kantord/toylang#83).\n813:             Type::Int64 => \"int64\".to_string(),\n814:             Type::Bool => \"bool\".to_string(),\n815:             // Same width as Int: a Char is a codepoint, and the checker already refuses to mix\n816:             // the two.\n817:             Type::Char => \"int32\".to_string(),\n818:             Type::Float => unreachable!(\"Float is JS-only in this row\"),\n819:             Type::Vec(e) => format!(\"[]{}\", self.go_type(e)),\n820:             Type::Enum { .. } if ty.as_opt().is_some() => {\n821:                 format!(\"tlOpt[{}]\", self.go_type(ty.as_opt().expect(\"guarded\")))\n822:             }\n823:             // Materialized eagerly as the slice of its entries, so it is a slice here too.\n824:             // Fusion is what will remove this materialization.\n825:             Type::Stream(e) => format!(\"[]{}\", self.go_type(e)),\n826:             Type::Record(_) => {\n827:                 let i = self\n828:                     .records\n829:                     .iter()\n830:                     .position(|r| r == ty)\n831:                     .expect(\"every record reachable from the program was collected\");\n832:                 format!(\"tlRec{i}\")\n833:             }\n834:             // The enum's identity -- name plus arguments -- names the struct: `ident()`\n835:             // embeds the arguments so each instantiation gets its own.\n836:             Type::Enum { .. } => format!(\"tlE_{}\", ty.ident()),\n837:             Type::Param(_) => unreachable!(\"params are substituted before emit\"),\n838:         }\n839:     }\n840: \n841:     /// The declaration index of `variant`, which is its tag and its pointer field's suffix.\n842:     /// A partial chain's yield is an Opt, so a present arm wraps its value in the tag\n843:     /// struct; total chains return the body bare. Split out of `expr`'s match arm to keep\n844:     /// the tagging decision from deepening it (kantord/toylang#62).\n845:     fn arm_yield(go_ty: String, body: String, partial: bool) -> String {\n846:         if partial {\n847:             format!(\"{go_ty}{{true, {body}}}\")\n848:         } else {\n849:             body\n850:         }\n851:     }\n852: \n853:     fn variant_index(variants: &[(String, Option<Type>)], variant: &str) -> usize {\n854:         variants\n855:             .iter()\n856:             .position(|(n, _)| n == variant)\n857:             .expect(\"the checker resolved the variant against this enum\")\n858:     }\n859: \n860:     /// The decoder for one enum: a bare string resolves among the unit variants, a single-key\n861:     /// object among the payload ones, and a near miss is refused naming the enum.\n862:     fn enum_unmarshal(&self, en: &Type, name: &str) -> String {\n863:         let variants = ty::variants(self.registry, en);\n864:         let ename = self.go_type(en);\n865:         let mut out = String::new();\n866:         out.push_str(&format!(\n867:             \"func (e *{ename}) UnmarshalJSON(b []byte) error {{\\n\"\n868:         ));\n869:         out.push_str(\"\\tvar s string\\n\");\n870:         out.push_str(\"\\tif json.Unmarshal(b, &s) == nil {\\n\");\n871:         out.push_str(\"\\t\\tswitch s {\\n\");\n872:         for (i, (vname, payload)) in variants.iter().enumerate() {\n873:             if payload.is_none() {\n874:                 out.push_str(&format!(\n875:                     \"\\t\\tcase \\\"{vname}\\\":\\n\\t\\t\\t*e = {ename}{{tag: {i}}}\\n\\t\\t\\treturn nil\\n\"\n876:                 ));\n877:             }\n878:         }\n879:         out.push_str(\"\\t\\t}\\n\");\n880:         out.push_str(&format!(\n881:             \"\\t\\treturn fmt.Errorf(\\\"`%s` is not a unit variant of {name}\\\", s)\\n\\t}}\\n\"\n882:         ));\n883:         let payloads: Vec<(usize, &String, &Type)> = variants\n884:             .iter()\n885:             .enumerate()\n886:             .filter_map(|(i, (v, p))| p.as_ref().map(|p| (i, v, p)))\n887:             .collect();\n888:         if payloads.is_empty() {\n889:             out.push_str(&format!(\"\\treturn fmt.Errorf(\\\"expected {name}\\\")\\n}}\\n\\n\"));\n890:             return out;\n891:         }\n892:         out.push_str(\"\\tvar m map[string]json.RawMessage\\n\");\n893:         out.push_str(\"\\tif err := json.Unmarshal(b, &m); err != nil || len(m) != 1 {\\n\");\n894:         out.push_str(&format!(\n895:             \"\\t\\treturn fmt.Errorf(\\\"expected {name}\\\")\\n\\t}}\\n\"\n896:         ));\n897:         out.push_str(\"\\tfor k, v := range m {\\n\");\n898:         out.push_str(\"\\t\\tswitch k {\\n\");\n899:         for (i, vname, pty) in &payloads {\n900:             out.push_str(&format!(\"\\t\\tcase \\\"{vname}\\\":\\n\"));\n901:             out.push_str(&format!(\"\\t\\t\\tvar p {}\\n\", self.go_type(pty)));\n902:             out.push_str(\"\\t\\t\\tif err := json.Unmarshal(v, &p); err != nil {\\n\\t\\t\\t\\treturn err\\n\\t\\t\\t}\\n\");\n903:             out.push_str(&format!(\n904:                 \"\\t\\t\\t*e = {ename}{{tag: {i}, p{i}: &p}}\\n\\t\\t\\treturn nil\\n\"\n905:             ));\n906:         }\n907:         out.push_str(\"\\t\\t}\\n\");\n908:         out.push_str(&format!(\n909:             \"\\t\\treturn fmt.Errorf(\\\"`%s` is not a payload variant of {name}\\\", k)\\n\\t}}\\n\"\n910:         ));\n911:         out.push_str(&format!(\"\\treturn fmt.Errorf(\\\"expected {name}\\\")\\n}}\\n\\n\"));\n912:         out\n913:     }\n914: \n915:     /// A `jsonlines(f(inputs))` program, compiled as a loop that decodes one JSON value at a\n916:     /// time (the same `json.Decoder`-until-`io.EOF` loop the eager `inputs` path already used,\n917:     /// just without collecting into a slice first) and prints it immediately. No explicit flush\n918:     /// is needed: unlike Rust, Python, jq and Lua, `fmt.Println` writes straight through to the\n919:     /// underlying `os.Stdout` file descriptor with no userspace buffering in between.\n920:     ///\n921:     /// Each stage's binding is followed by `_ = t_N`: it is a `:=` local, not a closure parameter\n922:     /// the way the eager path's `tlMap`/`tlSelect` callback argument is, and Go rejects an unused\n923:     /// local at compile time -- a `select` whose predicate is the only use, or a `map` whose body\n924:     /// ignores its argument entirely, would otherwise fail to build only in the fused case.\n925:     fn fused_main(&self, program: &Program, fusion: &tir::Fusion) -> String {\n926:         let mut out = String::new();\n927:         out.push_str(\"func main() {\\n\");\n928:         let (mut current, mut current_ty) = match fusion.source {\n929:             tir::Source::Inputs => {\n930:                 let elem = program\n931:                     .inputs\n932:                     .as_ref()\n933:                     .expect(\"an inputs source recorded its element\");\n934:                 out.push_str(\"\\tdec := json.NewDecoder(os.Stdin)\\n\");\n935:                 out.push_str(\"\\tfor {\\n\");\n936:                 out.push_str(&format!(\"\\t\\tvar t_line {}\\n\", self.go_type(elem)));\n937:                 out.push_str(\n938:                     \"\\t\\tif err := dec.Decode(&t_line); err != nil {\\n\\t\\t\\tif err == io.EOF {\\n\\t\\t\\t\\tbreak\\n\\t\\t\\t}\\n\\t\\t\\ttlFail(err.Error())\\n\\t\\t}\\n\",\n939:                 );\n940:                 (\"t_line\".to_string(), elem.clone())\n941:             }\n942:             // The same scanner the eager collect helper uses, one line per Scan; a raw line is\n943:             // already the element, blank ones included.\n944:             tir::Source::Lines => {\n945:                 out.push_str(\"\\ts := bufio.NewScanner(os.Stdin)\\n\");\n946:                 out.push_str(\"\\ts.Buffer(make([]byte, 0, 65536), 1024*1024)\\n\");\n947:                 out.push_str(\"\\ts.Split(tlScanLines)\\n\");\n948:                 out.push_str(\"\\tfor s.Scan() {\\n\");\n949:                 out.push_str(\"\\t\\tt_line := s.Text()\\n\");\n950:                 (\"t_line\".to_string(), Type::Str)\n951:             }\n952:             // The bound is evaluated once; the loop counter is the element. A negative bound\n953:             // clamps to zero, the same answer `tlRange` gives eagerly.\n954:             tir::Source::Range(bound) => {\n955:                 out.push_str(&format!(\"\\tn := {}\\n\", self.expr(bound)));\n956:                 out.push_str(\"\\tif n < 0 {\\n\\t\\tn = 0\\n\\t}\\n\");\n957:                 out.push_str(\"\\tfor t_i := int32(0); t_i < n; t_i++ {\\n\");\n958:                 (\"t_i\".to_string(), Type::Int)\n959:             }\n960:         };\n961:         for stage in &fusion.stages {\n962:             match stage {\n963:                 tir::Stage::Map { param, body } => {\n964:                     out.push_str(&format!(\n965:                         \"\\t\\t{} := {}\\n\\t\\t_ = {}\\n\",\n966:                         self.local(*param),\n967:                         current,\n968:                         self.local(*param)\n969:                     ));\n970:                     current = self.expr(body);\n971:                     current_ty = body.ty.clone();\n972:                 }\n973:                 tir::Stage::Select { param, pred } => {\n974:                     out.push_str(&format!(\n975:                         \"\\t\\t{} := {}\\n\\t\\t_ = {}\\n\",\n976:                         self.local(*param),\n977:                         current,\n978:                         self.local(*param)\n979:                     ));\n980:                     out.push_str(&format!(\n981:                         \"\\t\\tif !({}) {{\\n\\t\\t\\tcontinue\\n\\t\\t}}\\n\",\n982:                         self.expr(pred)\n983:                     ));\n984:                     current = self.local(*param);\n985:                 }\n986:             }\n987:         }\n988:         let printed = self.show(&current_ty, &current, 0);\n989:         out.push_str(&format!(\"\\t\\tfmt.Println({printed})\\n\"));\n990:         out.push_str(\"\\t}\\n}\\n\");\n991:         out\n992:     }\n993: \n994:     /// Wrap `leaf` in `depth` layers of `tlMap`, spelling the element type at each one.\n995:     ///\n996:     /// This is what the other backends get for free from a `depth` argument. Here the map at\n997:     /// each level is a different instantiation, so the types have to be written down -- and they\n998:     /// can be, because every node in the IR carries the type it was checked at.\n999:     fn distribute(\n1000:         &self,\n1001:         value: &str,\n1002:         value_ty: &Type,\n1003:         result_ty: &Type,\n1004:         depth: usize,\n1005:         leaf: &dyn Fn(&str) -> String,\n1006:     ) -> String {\n1007:         if depth == 0 {\n1008:             return leaf(value);\n1009:         }\n1010:         let elem = tir::runtime_elem(value_ty).expect(\"a dimension to distribute over\");\n1011:         let result_elem = tir::runtime_elem(result_ty).expect(\"the result keeps the dimension\");\n1012:         let var = format!(\"m{depth}\");\n1013:         format!(\n1014:             \"tlMap({value}, func({var} {}) {} {{ return {} }})\",\n1015:             self.go_type(elem),\n1016:             self.go_type(result_elem),\n1017:             self.distribute(&var, elem, result_elem, depth - 1, leaf)\n1018:         )\n1019:     }\n1020: \n1021:     fn expr(&self, t: &Tir) -> String {\n1022:         match &t.kind {\n1023:             Kind::Str(s) => go_string(s),\n1024:             Kind::Int(n) => int_lit(&t.ty, *n),\n1025:             Kind::Float(_) => unreachable!(\"Float is JS-only in this row\"),\n1026:             Kind::Var(name) => self.user(name),\n1027:             Kind::Local(id) => self.local(*id),\n1028:             Kind::Input => INPUT.to_string(),\n1029:             Kind::Inputs => INPUTS.to_string(),\n1030:             // The stream, materialized eagerly: whatever consumes it -- `collect`, a mapper --\n1031:             // works on the slice of its entries.\n1032:             Kind::Lines => \"tlCollectLines()\".to_string(),\n1033:             Kind::Dsv { delim } => format!(\n1034:                 \"tlDsv(tlCollectLines(), {})\",\n1035:                 go_string(delim)\n1036:             ),\n1037:             // go_type resolves the struct name, and the collector registered it because a\n1038:             // record literal carries its own record type.\n1039:             Kind::RecordLit { fields } => {\n1040:                 let parts: Vec<String> = fields\n1041:                     .iter()\n1042:                     .map(|(name, value)| format!(\"F{name}: {}\", self.expr(value)))\n1043:                     .collect();\n1044:                 format!(\"{}{{{}}}\", self.go_type(&t.ty), parts.join(\", \"))\n1045:             }\n1046: \n1047:             Kind::EnumLit { variant, payload } => {\n1048:                 // Opt's constructors build its tlOpt encoding, not a tag struct: `some(x)`\n1049:                 // is presence, `none` is the zero value.\n1050:                 if t.ty.as_opt().is_some() {\n1051:                     return match payload {\n1052:                         Some(p) => {\n1053:                             format!(\"{}{{true, {}}}\", self.go_type(&t.ty), self.expr(p))\n1054:                         }\n1055:                         None => format!(\"{}{{}}\", self.go_type(&t.ty)),\n1056:                     };\n1057:                 }\n1058:                 let i = Self::variant_index(&ty::variants(self.registry, &t.ty), variant);\n1059:                 match payload {\n1060:                     None => format!(\"{}{{tag: {i}}}\", self.go_type(&t.ty)),\n1061:                     Some(p) => format!(\n1062:                         \"{}{{tag: {i}, p{i}: tlPtr({})}}\",\n1063:                         self.go_type(&t.ty),\n1064:                         self.expr(p)\n1065:                     ),\n1066:                 }\n1067:             }\n1068: \n1069:             Kind::VecLit(items) => {\n1070:                 let parts: Vec<String> = items.iter().map(|i| self.expr(i)).collect();\n1071:                 format!(\"{}{{{}}}\", self.go_type(&t.ty), parts.join(\", \"))\n1072:             }\n1073:             Kind::Call { func, arg } => format!(\n1074:                 \"{}({})\",\n1075:                 self.user(func),\n1076:                 arg.as_deref().map_or_else(String::new, |a| self.expr(a))\n1077:             ),\n1078:             Kind::Concat(l, r) => self.concat(&t.ty, l, r),\n1079:             Kind::Arith { op, lhs, rhs } => arith(&t.ty, *op, self.expr(lhs), self.expr(rhs)),\n1080:             // Go's own `&&`/`||`, which short-circuit, so the right side stays unevaluated\n1081:             // exactly where toylang says it does.\n1082:             Kind::Logic { op, lhs, rhs } => {\n1083:                 let op = match op {\n1084:                     LogicOp::And => \"&&\",\n1085:                     LogicOp::Or => \"||\",\n1086:                 };\n1087:                 format!(\"({} {op} {})\", self.expr(lhs), self.expr(rhs))\n1088:             }\n1089:             Kind::Not(base) => format!(\"(!{})\", self.expr(base)),\n1090:             Kind::Builtin { which, arg } => match which {\n1091:                 Builtin::IntToStr => format!(\"strconv.FormatInt(int64({}), 10)\", self.expr(arg)),\n1092:                 Builtin::IntToI64 => format!(\"int64({})\", self.expr(arg)),\n1093:                 Builtin::Range => format!(\"tlRange({})\", self.expr(arg)),\n1094:                 Builtin::Chars => format!(\"tlChars({})\", self.expr(arg)),\n1095:                 Builtin::JsonLines => {\n1096:                     let elem = tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty).expect(\"checked to be a Vec or a stream\");\n1097:                     let e = \"e0\".to_string();\n1098:                     format!(\n1099:                         \"tlJsonlines({}, func({e} {}) string {{ return {} }})\",\n1100:                         self.expr(arg),\n1101:                         self.go_type(elem),\n1102:                         self.show(elem, &e, 1)\n1103:                     )\n1104:                 }\n1105:                 // The source already materialized, so the exit has nothing left to do.\n1106:                 Builtin::Collect => self.expr(arg),\n1107:                 Builtin::Length => format!(\"int32(len({}))\", self.expr(arg)),\n1108:                 Builtin::Tail => format!(\"tlTail({})\", self.expr(arg)),\n1109:                 Builtin::Flatten => format!(\"tlFlatten({})\", self.expr(arg)),\n1110:                 Builtin::Sort => format!(\"tlSort({})\", self.expr(arg)),\n1111:                 Builtin::Reverse => format!(\"tlReverse({})\", self.expr(arg)),\n1112:                 // Which fold depends on the element width: int32 and int64 are distinct types in\n1113:                 // Go, so they cannot share one helper the way a dynamically typed backend can.\n1114:                 Builtin::Sum => {\n1115:                     if tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty) == Some(&Type::Int) {\n1116:                         format!(\"tlSum({})\", self.expr(arg))\n1117:                     } else {\n1118:                         format!(\"tlSum64({})\", self.expr(arg))\n1119:                     }\n1120:                 }\n1121:                 Builtin::Max => format!(\"tlMax({})\", self.expr(arg)),\n1122:                 // The names come from the checked type, not the struct value, so `arg` runs in\n1123:                 // an ignored parameter -- the same IIFE shape `Bind` uses -- purely for whatever\n1124:                 // else it does.\n1125:                 Builtin::Fields => {\n1126:                     let Type::Record(fields) = &arg.ty else {\n1127:                         unreachable!(\"checked to be a record\")\n1128:                     };\n1129:                     let names: Vec<String> = fields.iter().map(|(n, _)| go_string(n)).collect();\n1130:                     format!(\n1131:                         \"func(_ {}) []string {{ return []string{{{}}} }}({})\",\n1132:                         self.go_type(&arg.ty),\n1133:                         names.join(\", \"),\n1134:                         self.expr(arg)\n1135:                     )\n1136:                 }\n1137:             },\n1138:             Kind::Compare { op, lhs, rhs } => self.compare(*op, lhs, rhs),\n1139:             Kind::Bind {\n1140:                 local: id,\n1141:                 value,\n1142:                 body,\n1143:             } => format!(\n1144:                 \"func({} {}) {} {{ return {} }}({})\",\n1145:                 self.local(*id),\n1146:                 self.go_type(&value.ty),\n1147:                 self.go_type(&t.ty),\n1148:                 self.expr(body),\n1149:                 self.expr(value)\n1150:             ),\n1151:             Kind::Map {\n1152:                 source,\n1153:                 param,\n1154:                 body,\n1155:             } => format!(\n1156:                 \"tlMap({}, func({} {}) {} {{ return {} }})\",\n1157:                 self.expr(source),\n1158:                 self.local(*param),\n1159:                 self.go_type(tir::runtime_elem(&source.ty).expect(\"map runs over a dimension\")),\n1160:                 self.go_type(&body.ty),\n1161:                 self.expr(body)\n1162:             ),\n1163:             Kind::Select {\n1164:                 source,\n1165:                 param,\n1166:                 pred,\n1167:             } => format!(\n1168:                 \"tlSelect({}, func({} {}) bool {{ return {} }})\",\n1169:                 self.expr(source),\n1170:                 self.local(*param),\n1171:                 self.go_type(tir::runtime_elem(&source.ty).expect(\"select runs over a dimension\")),\n1172:                 self.expr(pred)\n1173:             ),\n1174:             // Opt's reorder pass (kantord/toylang#66): the same `!o.ok`/`.v` shape tlUnwrap and\n1175:             // the printer already branch on, generalised to rebuild the tlOpt instead of\n1176:             // reading through it. `__srcOpt` binds the source once, so evaluating it twice (the\n1177:             // `.ok` test, then `.v`) costs nothing beyond a field read.\n1178:             Kind::OptMap {\n1179:                 source,\n1180:                 param,\n1181:                 body,\n1182:             } => format!(\n1183:                 \"func(__srcOpt {}) {} {{ if !__srcOpt.ok {{ return {1}{{}} }}; {} := __srcOpt.v; _ = {2}; return {1}{{true, {}}} }}({})\",\n1184:                 self.go_type(&source.ty),\n1185:                 self.go_type(&t.ty),\n1186:                 self.local(*param),\n1187:                 self.expr(body),\n1188:                 self.expr(source)\n1189:             ),\n1190:             Kind::Field { base, name } => {\n1191:                 let depth = tir::vec_depth(&base.ty);\n1192:                 self.distribute(&self.expr(base), &base.ty, &t.ty, depth, &|v| {\n1193:                     format!(\"({v}).F{name}\")\n1194:                 })\n1195:             }\n1196:             Kind::Unwrap { base } => {\n1197:                 let depth = tir::vec_depth(&base.ty);\n1198:                 self.distribute(&self.expr(base), &base.ty, &t.ty, depth, &|v| {\n1199:                     format!(\"tlUnwrap({v})\")\n1200:                 })\n1201:             }\n1202:             Kind::Index {\n1203:                 base, index, depth, ..\n1204:             } => {\n1205:                 let i = self.expr(index);\n1206:                 self.distribute(&self.expr(base), &base.ty, &t.ty, *depth, &|v| {\n1207:                     format!(\"tlAt({v}, {i})\")\n1208:                 })\n1209:             }\n1210:             Kind::Slice {\n1211:                 base, start, end, depth,\n1212:             } => {\n1213:                 let lo = match start {\n1214:                     Some(s) => self.expr(s),\n1215:                     None => \"-2147483648\".to_string(),\n1216:                 };\n1217:                 let hi = match end {\n1218:                     Some(e) => self.expr(e),\n1219:                     None => \"2147483647\".to_string(),\n1220:                 };\n1221:                 self.distribute(&self.expr(base), &base.ty, &t.ty, *depth, &|v| {\n1222:                     format!(\"tlSlice({v}, {lo}, {hi})\")\n1223:                 })\n1224:             }\n1225:             // Tests over the subject (a plain local, so re-reading it is free): a tag test for\n1226:             // a variant arm, the same chain the enum printer uses, and the guard's own Bool\n1227:             // for a guard arm. A total chain's last arm needs no test, the checker having\n1228:             // proved nothing else can reach it; a partial chain tests every arm, wraps each\n1229:             // body in the present Opt, and falls through to the absent one. `_ =` after a\n1230:             // payload binding, as in the fused loop, because Go rejects an unused local and a\n1231:             // body is free to ignore its payload.\n1232:             Kind::Match {\n1233:                 subject,\n1234:                 arms,\n1235:                 partial,\n1236:             } => {\n1237:                 let subj = self.expr(subject);\n1238:                 let mut body = String::new();\n1239:                 for (i, arm) in arms.iter().enumerate() {\n1240:                     let mut run = String::new();\n1241:                     if let Some(pid) = arm.payload {\n1242:                         let variant = arm\n1243:                             .variant\n1244:                             .as_ref()\n1245:                             .expect(\"only a variant arm has a payload\");\n1246:                         let vi =\n1247:                             Self::variant_index(&ty::variants(self.registry, &subject.ty), variant);\n1248:                         run.push_str(&format!(\n1249:                             \"{} := *{subj}.p{vi}; _ = {}; \",\n1250:                             self.local(pid),\n1251:                             self.local(pid)\n1252:                         ));\n1253:                     }\n1254:                     let produced =\n1255:                         Self::arm_yield(self.go_type(&t.ty), self.expr(&arm.body), *partial);\n1256:                     run.push_str(&format!(\"return {produced}\"));\n1257:                     let test = match (&arm.variant, &arm.guard) {\n1258:                         (Some(v), _) => Some(format!(\n1259:                             \"{subj}.tag == {}\",\n1260:                             Self::variant_index(&ty::variants(self.registry, &subject.ty), v)\n1261:                         )),\n1262:                         (None, Some(g)) => Some(self.expr(g)),\n1263:                         (None, None) => None,\n1264:                     };\n1265:                     match test {\n1266:                         Some(test) if *partial || i + 1 < arms.len() => {\n1267:                             body.push_str(&format!(\"if {test} {{ {run} }}; \"));\n1268:                         }\n1269:                         _ => body.push_str(&run),\n1270:                     }\n1271:                 }\n1272:                 if *partial {\n1273:                     body.push_str(&format!(\"return {}{{}}\", self.go_type(&t.ty)));\n1274:                 }\n1275:                 format!(\"func() {} {{ {body} }}()\", self.go_type(&t.ty))\n1276:             }\n1277:         }\n1278:     }\n1279: \n1280:     /// Go has no `+` for slices, so a Vec joins through the same helper `flatten` uses, wrapped\n1281:     /// around a two-entry outer literal rather than through a second helper.\n1282:     fn concat(&self, ty: &Type, l: &Tir, r: &Tir) -> String {\n1283:         match ty {\n1284:             Type::Vec(_) => {\n1285:                 let go_ty = self.go_type(ty);\n1286:                 let (l, r) = (self.expr(l), self.expr(r));\n1287:                 format!(\"tlFlatten([]{go_ty}{{{l}, {r}}})\")\n1288:             }\n1289:             _ => format!(\"({} + {})\", self.expr(l), self.expr(r)),\n1290:         }\n1291:     }\n1292: \n1293:     /// The printer is built from the type rather than by inspecting the value, as on every other\n1294:     /// backend. Here there is no choice at all: a Go value cannot be asked what it is.\n1295:     /// Equality on a composite is structural, which Go's own `==` is not: it compares an enum's\n1296:     /// payload pointer by address (kantord/toylang#68). Ordering on a composite is a separate\n1297:     /// open question and keeps whatever Go does with it.\n1298:     fn compare(&self, op: BinOp, lhs: &Tir, rhs: &Tir) -> String {\n1299:         if !lhs.ty.is_composite() || !matches!(op, BinOp::Eq | BinOp::Ne) {\n1300:             return format!(\"({} {} {})\", self.expr(lhs), go_op(op), self.expr(rhs));\n1301:         }\n1302:         let call = format!(\"tlEq({}, {})\", self.expr(lhs), self.expr(rhs));\n1303:         match op {\n1304:             BinOp::Ne => format!(\"(!{call})\"),\n1305:             _ => call,\n1306:         }\n1307:     }\n1308: \n1309:     fn show(&self, ty: &Type, value: &str, depth: usize) -> String {\n1310:         match ty {\n1311:             Type::Param(_) => unreachable!(\"params are substituted before emit\"),\n1312:             // The checker refuses a program whose result contains a stream, since there is\n1313:             // nothing to print: a stream has no value, only a promise that collect can redeem.\n1314:             Type::Stream(_) => unreachable!(\"a stream cannot reach the printer\"),\n1315:             Type::Char => unreachable!(\"Char cannot reach the printer, refused by the checker\"),\n1316:             Type::Str => format!(\"tlQuote({value})\"),\n1317:             Type::Sink => unreachable!(\"a sink only ever prints raw, never through the printer\"),\n1318:             Type::Int => format!(\"strconv.FormatInt(int64({value}), 10)\"),\n1319:             Type::Int64 => format!(\"strconv.FormatInt({value}, 10)\"),\n1320:             Type::Float => unreachable!(\"Float is JS-only in this row\"),\n\n(Output capped at 50 KB. Showing lines 1-1320. Use offset=1321 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"//! The Go backend.\n//!\n//! Go is the first target that is both statically typed and has no runtime type information to\n//! fall back on, and that changes what an emitter has to do. Lua, JavaScript and jq all accept a\n//! depth-polymorphic helper -- one `tl_field(v, k, depth)` serves every shape -- because the\n//! value carries its own type. LLVM sidesteps the same problem by erasing everything into\n//! `tl_vec`. Go will not do either: a `[]User` and a `[][]User` are different types, so the\n//! distribution over dimensions has to be spelled out at each use site, with the intermediate\n//! types written in. The checker computed those types already, so what looks like extra work is\n//! really the first target that asks for what was always there.\n//!\n//! The second thing Go asks for is a *name*. A record type is structural here and anonymous\n//! everywhere else; Go needs a declared struct before a value of one can exist, so every record\n//! the program mentions is collected up front and given one.\n\nuse std::collections::BTreeSet;\n\nuse crate::ast::{BinOp, LogicOp};\nuse crate::tir::{self, Builtin, Kind, LocalId, Program, Tir};\nuse crate::ty::{self, Enums, Type};","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-go/src/emit_go.rs","text":"//! The Go backend.\n//!\n//! Go is the first target that is both statically typed and has no runtime type information to\n//! fall back on, and that changes what an emitter has to do. Lua, JavaScript and jq all accept a\n//! depth-polymorphic helper -- one `tl_field(v, k, depth)` serves every shape -- because the\n//! value carries its own type. LLVM sidesteps the same problem by erasing everything into\n//! `tl_vec`. Go will not do either: a `[]User` and a `[][]User` are different types, so the\n//! distribution over dimensions has to be spelled out at each use site, with the intermediate\n//! types written in. The checker computed those types already, so what looks like extra work is\n//! really the first target that asks for what was always there.\n//!\n//! The second thing Go asks for is a *name*. A record type is structural here and anonymous\n//! everywhere else; Go needs a declared struct before a value of one can exist, so every record\n//! the program mentions is collected up front and given one.\n\nuse std::collections::BTreeSet;\n\nuse crate::ast::{BinOp, LogicOp};\nuse crate::tir::{self, Builtin, Kind, LocalId, Program, Tir};\nuse crate::ty::{self, Enums, Type};\n\n/// The binding the input value is read into. Unspellable in source, since every source name is\n/// prefixed.\nconst INPUT: &str = \"t_input\";\nconst INPUTS: &str = \"t_inputs\";\n\n/// Absence is a field rather than a nil pointer: `Opt<Vec<T>>` would otherwise have two\n/// spellings of empty, and a nil slice is a perfectly ordinary present value here.\nconst OPT_TYPE: &str = r#\"type tlOpt[T any] struct {\n\tok bool\n\tv  T\n}\n\"#;\n\nconst FAIL_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlFail(msg string) {\n\tfmt.Fprintln(os.Stderr, \"toylang: \"+msg)\n\tos.Exit(1)\n}\n\"#;\n\nconst MAP_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlMap[A, B any](src []A, f func(A) B) []B {\n\tout := make([]B, len(src))\n\tfor i, e := range src {\n\t\tout[i] = f(e)\n\t}\n\treturn out\n}\n\"#;\n\nconst SELECT_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlSelect[T any](src []T, pred func(T) bool) []T {\n\tout := []T{}\n\tfor _, e := range src {\n\t\tif pred(e) {\n\t\t\tout = append(out, e)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn out\n}\n\"#;\n\nconst AT_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlAt[T any](v []T, i int32) tlOpt[T] {\n\tn := int32(len(v))\n\tif i < 0 {\n\t\ti = n + i\n\t}\n\tif i < 0 || i >= n {\n\t\treturn tlOpt[T]{}\n\t}\n\treturn tlOpt[T]{true, v[i]}\n}\n\"#;\n\n// Go's own slicing panics out of range, so the jq clamp has to be explicit: negatives count\n// from the end, then both bounds clamp to [0, n], and a crossed window is empty. A bound left\n// out is passed as its sentinel (`MinInt32` for the start, `MaxInt32` for the end), each of\n// which the clamp folds to the array's own boundary.\nconst SLICE_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlSlice[T any](v []T, lo int32, hi int32) []T {\n\tn := int32(len(v))\n\tif lo < 0 {\n\t\tlo += n\n\t}\n\tif hi < 0 {\n\t\thi += n\n\t}\n\tif lo < 0 {\n\t\tlo = 0\n\t}\n\tif lo > n {\n\t\tlo = n\n\t}\n\tif hi < 0 {\n\t\thi = 0\n\t}\n\tif hi > n {\n\t\thi = n\n\t}\n\tif lo >= hi {\n\t\treturn []T{}\n\t}\n\treturn v[lo:hi]\n}\n\"#;\n\nconst UNWRAP_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlUnwrap[T any](o tlOpt[T]) T {\n\tif !o.ok {\n\t\ttlFail(\"unwrapped a value that is not there\")\n\t}\n\treturn o.v\n}\n\"#;\n\nconst TAIL_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlTail[T any](v []T) tlOpt[[]T] {\n\tif len(v) == 0 {\n\t\treturn tlOpt[[]T]{}\n\t}\n\treturn tlOpt[[]T]{true, v[1:]}\n}\n\"#;\n\nconst FLATTEN_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlFlatten[T any](vv [][]T) []T {\n\tout := []T{}\n\tfor _, v := range vv {\n\t\tout = append(out, v...)\n\t}\n\treturn out\n}\n\"#;\n\n// `cmp.Ordered` is exactly the constraint the checker's own `orderable` restricts `sort`'s\n// element type to, so nothing here has to name Int, Int64, Str, or Char individually.\nconst SORT_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlSort[T cmp.Ordered](v []T) []T {\n\tout := append([]T{}, v...)\n\tslices.Sort(out)\n\treturn out\n}\n\"#;\n\nconst REVERSE_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlReverse[T any](v []T) []T {\n\tout := make([]T, len(v))\n\tfor i, x := range v {\n\t\tout[len(v)-1-i] = x\n\t}\n\treturn out\n}\n\"#;\n\n// `int32`/`int64` `+=` wraps by definition, so a fold needs nothing but the loop itself -- the\n// same \"the width wraps on its own\" story ARITH_HELPER and ARITH64_HELPER tell for one operator.\nconst SUM_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlSum(v []int32) int32 {\n\tvar acc int32\n\tfor _, x := range v {\n\t\tacc += x\n\t}\n\treturn acc\n}\n\nfunc tlSum64(v []int64) int64 {\n\tvar acc int64\n\tfor _, x := range v {\n\t\tacc += x\n\t}\n\treturn acc\n}\n\"#;\n\n// `cmp.Ordered` covers both integer widths, so one helper serves Int and Int64. Absence is the\n// zero `tlOpt`, the same answer an out-of-range index gives.\nconst MAX_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlMax[T cmp.Ordered](v []T) tlOpt[T] {\n\tif len(v) == 0 {\n\t\treturn tlOpt[T]{}\n\t}\n\tm := v[0]\n\tfor _, x := range v[1:] {\n\t\tif x > m {\n\t\t\tm = x\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn tlOpt[T]{true, m}\n}\n\"#;\n\n/// Go's `int32` wraps on overflow by definition, and its `/` and `%` truncate toward zero, so\n/// only the zero divisor needs a guard. `MIN / -1` is defined to be `MIN` here, which is the\n/// wrapping answer the other backends were made to give.\nconst ARITH_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlDiv(a, b int32) int32 {\n\tif b == 0 {\n\t\ttlFail(\"divided by zero\")\n\t}\n\treturn a / b\n}\n\nfunc tlRem(a, b int32) int32 {\n\tif b == 0 {\n\t\ttlFail(\"divided by zero\")\n\t}\n\treturn a % b\n}\n\"#;\n\n/// The same shape at 64 bits (kantord/toylang#83): Go defines `MIN / -1` as `MIN` for int64\n/// exactly as for int32, so only the zero divisor needs the guard here too.\nconst ARITH64_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlDiv64(a, b int64) int64 {\n\tif b == 0 {\n\t\ttlFail(\"divided by zero\")\n\t}\n\treturn a / b\n}\n\nfunc tlRem64(a, b int64) int64 {\n\tif b == 0 {\n\t\ttlFail(\"divided by zero\")\n\t}\n\treturn a % b\n}\n\"#;\n\n// An enum value carries its payload behind a pointer (tlPtr below), so Go's own `==` on two\n// of them compares addresses: `circle{r: 1} == circle{r: 1}` was false here and true on\n// Python (kantord/toylang#68). reflect.DeepEqual is the structural walk, following the\n// pointer and comparing what it points at.\nconst EQ_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlEq[T any](a, b T) bool { return reflect.DeepEqual(a, b) }\n\"#;\n\n/// Ad-hoc address-of: `&expr` only works on composite literals, and a payload is whatever\n/// expression the program wrote. The call inlines away.\nconst PTR_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlPtr[T any](v T) *T { return &v }\n\"#;\n\n/// Go folds constant arithmetic exactly and rejects a result that does not fit, which is the\n/// direct opposite of the wrapping rule: `2147483647 + 1` is a compile error rather than\n/// `-2147483648`. Passing every literal through a function makes the expression non-constant, so\n/// the wrap happens at runtime where it is defined. The call inlines away.\nconst INT_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlInt(n int32) int32 { return n }\n\"#;\n\n/// The same constant-folding escape at 64 bits: `tlInt64(9223372036854775807) + tlInt64(1)`\n/// must wrap at runtime rather than fail Go's exact constant arithmetic.\nconst INT64_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlInt64(n int64) int64 { return n }\n\"#;\n\n// bufio.ScanLines strips a trailing \\r along with the \\n, which the other five backends do\n// not, so the split function is copied here with that one line removed rather than reused.\nconst COLLECT_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlScanLines(data []byte, atEOF bool) (advance int, token []byte, err error) {\n\tif atEOF && len(data) == 0 {\n\t\treturn 0, nil, nil\n\t}\n\tif i := bytes.IndexByte(data, '\\n'); i >= 0 {\n\t\treturn i + 1, data[0:i], nil\n\t}\n\tif atEOF {\n\t\treturn len(data), data, nil\n\t}\n\treturn 0, nil, nil\n}\n\nfunc tlCollectLines() []string {\n\tout := []string{}\n\ts := bufio.NewScanner(os.Stdin)\n\ts.Buffer(make([]byte, 0, 65536), 1024*1024)\n\ts.Split(tlScanLines)\n\tfor s.Scan() {\n\t\tout = append(out, s.Text())\n\t}\n\treturn out\n}\n\"#;\n\n// RFC 4180 field splitting over the raw lines `tlCollectLines` keeps: a field wrapped in double\n// quotes may contain the delimiter, a doubled `\"\"` is a literal quote, and a quoted field may\n// span lines. The delimiter is matched literally, never as a pattern.\nconst DSV_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlDsv(lines []string, sep string) [][]string {\n\tout := [][]string{}\n\trow := []string{}\n\tfield := \"\"\n\tinQuotes := false\n\tfor _, line := range lines {\n\t\ti := 0\n\t\tfor i < len(line) {\n\t\t\tif inQuotes {\n\t\t\t\tif line[i] == '\"' {\n\t\t\t\t\tif i+1 < len(line) && line[i+1] == '\"' {\n\t\t\t\t\t\tfield += \"\\\"\"\n\t\t\t\t\t\ti += 2\n\t\t\t\t\t} else {\n\t\t\t\t\t\tinQuotes = false\n\t\t\t\t\t\ti++\n\t\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\t} else {\n\t\t\t\t\t_, size := utf8.DecodeRuneInString(line[i:])\n\t\t\t\t\tfield += line[i : i+size]\n\t\t\t\t\ti += size\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t} else if strings.HasPrefix(line[i:], sep) {\n\t\t\t\trow = append(row, field)\n\t\t\t\tfield = \"\"\n\t\t\t\ti += len(sep)\n\t\t\t} else if line[i] == '\"' && field == \"\" {\n\t\t\t\tinQuotes = true\n\t\t\t\ti++\n\t\t\t} else {\n\t\t\t\t_, size := utf8.DecodeRuneInString(line[i:])\n\t\t\t\tfield += line[i : i+size]\n\t\t\t\ti += size\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tif inQuotes {\n\t\t\tfield += \"\\n\"\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\trow = append(row, field)\n\t\t\tout = append(out, row)\n\t\t\trow = []string{}\n\t\t\tfield = \"\"\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif inQuotes {\n\t\tfield = field[:len(field)-1]\n\t\trow = append(row, field)\n\t\tout = append(out, row)\n\t}\n\treturn out\n}\n\"#;\n\nconst RANGE_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlRange(n int32) []int32 {\n\tif n < 0 {\n\t\tn = 0\n\t}\n\tout := make([]int32, n)\n\tfor i := int32(0); i < n; i++ {\n\t\tout[i] = i\n\t}\n\treturn out\n}\n\"#;\n\n// Go's `for range` over a string already decodes UTF-8 into runes -- Unicode scalar values --\n// rather than bytes, so there is no decoding to get right here.\nconst CHARS_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlChars(s string) []int32 {\n\tout := []int32{}\n\tfor _, r := range s {\n\t\tout = append(out, int32(r))\n\t}\n\treturn out\n}\n\"#;\n\nconst JSONLINES_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlJsonlines[T any](v []T, f func(T) string) string {\n\tparts := make([]string, len(v))\n\tfor i, e := range v {\n\t\tparts[i] = f(e)\n\t}\n\treturn strings.Join(parts, \"\\n\")\n}\n\"#;\n\nconst JOIN_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlJoin[T any](v []T, f func(T) string) string {\n\tparts := make([]string, len(v))\n\tfor i, e := range v {\n\t\tparts[i] = f(e)\n\t}\n\treturn \"[\" + strings.Join(parts, \",\") + \"]\"\n}\n\"#;\n\n/// Hand-written rather than `json.Marshal`, which escapes `<`, `>` and `&` and would print a\n/// different string than the other four backends for the same value.\nconst QUOTE_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlQuote(s string) string {\n\tvar b strings.Builder\n\tb.WriteByte('\"')\n\tfor i := 0; i < len(s); i++ {\n\t\tc := s[i]\n\t\tswitch c {\n\t\tcase '\"':\n\t\t\tb.WriteString(\"\\\\\\\"\")\n\t\tcase '\\\\':\n\t\t\tb.WriteString(\"\\\\\\\\\")\n\t\tcase '\\n':\n\t\t\tb.WriteString(\"\\\\n\")\n\t\tcase '\\r':\n\t\t\tb.WriteString(\"\\\\r\")\n\t\tcase '\\t':\n\t\t\tb.WriteString(\"\\\\t\")\n\t\tdefault:\n\t\t\tif c < 0x20 {\n\t\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(&b, \"\\\\u%04x\", c)\n\t\t\t} else {\n\t\t\t\tb.WriteByte(c)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tb.WriteByte('\"')\n\treturn b.String()\n}\n\"#;\n\npub fn emit(program: &Program) -> String {\n    let mut used = Used::default();\n    let mut records = Vec::new();\n    let mut enums = Vec::new();\n    let mut ctx = Collect {\n        used: &mut used,\n        records: &mut records,\n        enums: &mut enums,\n        registry: &program.enums,\n    };\n    for f in &program.funcs {\n        if let Some(param_ty) = &f.param_ty {\n            ctx.ty(param_ty);\n        }\n        ctx.walk(&f.body);\n    }\n    ctx.walk(&program.body);\n    if let Some(ty) = &program.input {\n        ctx.ty(ty);\n    }\n    if let Some(ty) = &program.inputs {\n        ctx.ty(ty);\n    }\n    records.sort_by_key(|t| t.to_string());\n    enums.sort_by_key(|t| t.to_string());\n\n    let e = Emitter {\n        records,\n        enums,\n        registry: &program.enums,\n    };\n\n    let mut decls = String::new();\n    for (i, rec) in e.records.iter().enumerate() {\n        let Type::Record(fields) = rec else {\n            unreachable!(\"only records are collected\")\n        };\n        decls.push_str(&format!(\"type tlRec{i} struct {{\\n\"));\n        for (name, ty) in fields {\n            // Exported, because encoding/json only fills fields it can see. The `F` prefix keeps\n            // the mapping injective, so two toylang fields cannot collide on one Go field.\n            decls.push_str(&format!(\"\\tF{name} {} `json:\\\"{name}\\\"`\\n\", e.go_type(ty)));\n        }\n        decls.push_str(\"}\\n\\n\");\n    }\n\n    // Go has no sum type, so an enum is a tag (the variant's declaration index) plus one\n    // pointer per payload variant, nil except for the one the tag names.\n    for en in &e.enums {\n        let Type::Enum { name, .. } = en else {\n            unreachable!(\"only enums are collected\")\n        };\n        decls.push_str(&format!(\"type {} struct {{\\n\\ttag int32\\n\", e.go_type(en)));\n        for (i, (_, payload)) in ty::variants(&program.enums, en).iter().enumerate() {\n            if let Some(p) = payload {\n                decls.push_str(&format!(\"\\tp{i} *{}\\n\", e.go_type(p)));\n            }\n        }\n        decls.push_str(\"}\\n\\n\");\n        // encoding/json cannot guess which of the enum's two wire shapes (ADR 0009) it is\n        // looking at, so decoding is spelled out. Only a program that reads input decodes.\n        if program.input.is_some() || program.inputs.is_some() {\n            decls.push_str(&e.enum_unmarshal(en, name));\n        }\n    }\n\n    decls.push_str(&e.printers(program));\n\n    // Package-level functions are visible in any order, so the forward reference the checker\n    // accepts needs nothing here. Lua wanted declarations and JavaScript relied on hoisting.\n    for f in &program.funcs {\n        let param = match (&f.param, &f.param_ty) {\n            (Some(name), Some(ty)) => format!(\"{} {}\", e.user(name), e.go_type(ty)),\n            (None, None) => String::new(),\n            _ => unreachable!(\"a function's param and param_ty agree\"),\n        };\n        decls.push_str(&format!(\n            \"func {}({}) {} {{\\n\\treturn {}\\n}}\\n\\n\",\n            e.user(&f.name),\n            param,\n            e.go_type(&f.body.ty),\n            e.expr(&f.body)\n        ));\n    }\n\n    if let Some(fusion) = tir::fusion(program) {\n        decls.push_str(&e.fused_main(program, &fusion));\n    } else {\n        decls.push_str(\"func main() {\\n\");\n        if let Some(ty) = &program.input {\n            decls.push_str(&format!(\"\\tvar {INPUT} {}\\n\", e.go_type(ty)));\n            decls.push_str(&format!(\n                \"\\tif err := json.NewDecoder(os.Stdin).Decode(&{INPUT}); err != nil {{\\n\\t\\ttlFail(err.Error())\\n\\t}}\\n\"\n            ));\n        }\n        if let Some(ty) = &program.inputs {\n            // Decode in a loop until io.EOF rather than reading one array token: the wire format\n            // is consecutive top-level JSON values, one per line, not a single `[...]`.\n            decls.push_str(&format!(\n                \"\\tvar {INPUTS} []{}\\n\\t{{\\n\\t\\tdec := json.NewDecoder(os.Stdin)\\n\\t\\tfor {{\\n\\t\\t\\tvar item {}\\n\\t\\t\\tif err := dec.Decode(&item); err != nil {{\\n\\t\\t\\t\\tif err == io.EOF {{\\n\\t\\t\\t\\t\\tbreak\\n\\t\\t\\t\\t}}\\n\\t\\t\\t\\ttlFail(err.Error())\\n\\t\\t\\t}}\\n\\t\\t\\t{INPUTS} = append({INPUTS}, item)\\n\\t\\t}}\\n\\t}}\\n\",\n                e.go_type(ty),\n                e.go_type(ty)\n            ));\n        }\n        let body = e.expr(&program.body);\n        // A top-level Str prints raw, the way jq's -r does; anything else prints as JSON.\n        let printed = if matches!(program.body.ty, Type::Str | Type::Sink) {\n            body\n        } else {\n            e.show(&program.body.ty, &body, 0)\n        };\n        decls.push_str(&format!(\"\\tfmt.Println({printed})\\n}}\\n\"));\n    }\n\n    // Which helpers to include is read off the emitted text rather than tracked alongside it. An\n    // unused function is not an error in Go, so a false positive costs nothing, while a missed\n    // one would not compile -- the asymmetry is what makes reading the output safe. Imports are\n    // the opposite (an unused one is an error), so those come from the walk instead.\n    let uses = |name: &str| decls.contains(name);\n    let unwrap = uses(\"tlUnwrap(\");\n    let arith = uses(\"tlDiv(\") || uses(\"tlRem(\");\n    let arith64 = uses(\"tlDiv64(\") || uses(\"tlRem64(\");\n    let collect = uses(\"tlCollectLines(\") || uses(\"tlScanLines\");\n    let fail = unwrap || arith || arith64 || program.input.is_some() || program.inputs.is_some();\n    let quote = uses(\"tlQuote(\");\n    let join = uses(\"tlJoin(\");\n\n    let mut helpers = String::new();\n    // tlOpt is what tlAt and tlUnwrap are written in terms of, and inference means the emitted\n    // text need never spell it. Helper-to-helper dependencies are stated rather than read back.\n    if uses(\"tlOpt[\") || uses(\"tlAt(\") || uses(\"tlTail(\") || unwrap {\n        helpers.push_str(OPT_TYPE);\n        helpers.push('\\n');\n    }\n    for (on, text) in [\n        (fail, FAIL_HELPER),\n        (uses(\"tlPtr(\"), PTR_HELPER),\n        (uses(\"tlEq(\"), EQ_HELPER),\n        (uses(\"tlInt(\"), INT_HELPER),\n        (uses(\"tlInt64(\"), INT64_HELPER),\n        (uses(\"tlMap(\"), MAP_HELPER),\n        (uses(\"tlSelect(\"), SELECT_HELPER),\n        (uses(\"tlAt(\"), AT_HELPER),\n        (uses(\"tlSlice(\"), SLICE_HELPER),\n        (uses(\"tlTail(\"), TAIL_HELPER),\n        (uses(\"tlFlatten(\"), FLATTEN_HELPER),\n        (uses(\"tlSort(\"), SORT_HELPER),\n        (uses(\"tlReverse(\"), REVERSE_HELPER),\n        (uses(\"tlSum(\") || uses(\"tlSum64(\"), SUM_HELPER),\n        (uses(\"tlMax(\"), MAX_HELPER),\n        (unwrap, UNWRAP_HELPER),\n        (arith, ARITH_HELPER),\n        (arith64, ARITH64_HELPER),\n        (uses(\"tlRange(\"), RANGE_HELPER),\n        (uses(\"tlChars(\"), CHARS_HELPER),\n        (collect, COLLECT_HELPER),\n        (uses(\"tlDsv(\"), DSV_HELPER),\n        (used.jsonlines, JSONLINES_HELPER),\n        (join, JOIN_HELPER),\n        (quote, QUOTE_HELPER),\n    ] {\n        if on {\n            helpers.push_str(text);\n            helpers.push('\\n');\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Go rejects an unused import, so each one is gated on the thing that spells it. Written as\n    // a table rather than a chain of `if`s, the same shape the helper text above already uses.\n    let reads_stdin = program.input.is_some() || program.inputs.is_some();\n    let mut imports = BTreeSet::new();\n    imports.insert(\"fmt\");\n    for (on, names) in [\n        (fail || reads_stdin || collect, &[\"os\"][..]),\n        (collect, &[\"bufio\", \"bytes\"]),\n        (reads_stdin, &[\"encoding/json\"]),\n        (program.inputs.is_some(), &[\"io\"]),\n        (join || quote || used.jsonlines || uses(\"tlDsv(\"), &[\"strings\"]),\n        (uses(\"tlDsv(\"), &[\"unicode/utf8\"]),\n        (uses(\"tlSort(\"), &[\"cmp\", \"slices\"]),\n        (uses(\"tlMax(\"), &[\"cmp\"]),\n        (uses(\"tlEq(\"), &[\"reflect\"]),\n        (\n            used.itoa\n                || used.jsonlines_has_scalar\n                || (program.body.ty != Type::Str && has_scalar(&program.enums, &program.body.ty)),\n            &[\"strconv\"],\n        ),\n    ] {\n        if on {\n            imports.extend(names);\n        }\n    }\n\n    let mut out = String::from(\"package main\\n\\nimport (\\n\");\n    for name in &imports {\n        out.push_str(&format!(\"\\t\\\"{name}\\\"\\n\"));\n    }\n    out.push_str(\")\\n\\n\");\n    out.push_str(&helpers);\n    out.push_str(&decls);\n    out\n}\n\n/// Whether printing this type reaches an Int or a Bool, which are the two `strconv` needs.\nfn has_scalar(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type) -> bool {\n    /// `seen` is what a recursive enum needs: its own payload leads back to it, and an enum\n    /// already under consideration answers nothing new.\n    fn reaches(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type, seen: &mut Vec<Type>) -> bool {\n        match ty {\n            // Only ever called on the program's own result type, which the checker guarantees is\n            // never a stream and never contains one.\n            Type::Stream(_) => unreachable!(\"a stream cannot reach has_scalar\"),\n            // The checker refuses a program whose result contains a Char, the same as a stream.\n            Type::Char => unreachable!(\"a Char cannot reach has_scalar\"),\n            Type::Int | Type::Int64 | Type::Bool => true,\n            // A Float prints as a plain number, which needs no strconv import.\n            Type::Float => false,\n            Type::Str => false,\n            Type::Sink => false,\n            Type::Vec(t) => reaches(enums, t, seen),\n            Type::Record(fields) => fields.iter().any(|(_, t)| reaches(enums, t, seen)),\n            Type::Enum { .. } => {\n                if seen.contains(ty) {\n                    return false;\n                }\n                seen.push(ty.clone());\n                ty::variants(enums, ty)\n                    .iter()\n                    .any(|(_, p)| p.as_ref().is_some_and(|p| reaches(enums, p, seen)))\n            }\n            Type::Param(_) => unreachable!(\"params are substituted before emit\"),\n        }\n    }\n    reaches(enums, ty, &mut Vec::new())\n}\n\n#[derive(Default)]\nstruct Used {\n    itoa: bool,\n    /// Whether `jsonlines` was called at all, which decides whether `strings` needs importing.\n    jsonlines: bool,\n    /// Whether `strconv` is needed for a scalar inside a `jsonlines` element type, which the\n    /// ordinary `has_scalar` check on the program's own result type misses whenever that result\n    /// is exactly `Str` -- true for every `jsonlines` call, since that is what it returns.\n    jsonlines_has_scalar: bool,\n}\n\n/// One walk, collecting the record types that need declaring and the two builtins whose imports\n/// cannot be read back off the emitted text without risking a false positive.\nstruct Collect<'a> {\n    used: &'a mut Used,\n    records: &'a mut Vec<Type>,\n    enums: &'a mut Vec<Type>,\n    /// Every enum the program declared. The variant list on a `Type::Enum` in hand may be a\n    /// placeholder, so the payloads to descend into are read from here (`ty::variants`).\n    registry: &'a Enums,\n}\n\nimpl Collect<'_> {\n    fn ty(&mut self, t: &Type) {\n        match t {\n            Type::Vec(e) | Type::Stream(e) => self.ty(e),\n            Type::Record(fields) => {\n                if !self.records.contains(t) {\n                    self.records.push(t.clone());\n                }\n                for (_, f) in fields {\n                    self.ty(f);\n                }\n            }\n            // Opt keeps the tlOpt[T] struct it always had (already tagged), so it is not\n            // harvested as a declared enum; only what it holds is.\n            Type::Enum { .. } => {\n                if let Some(inner) = t.as_opt() {\n                    self.ty(inner);\n                    return;\n                }\n                // Having it already is what stops a recursive enum here: its own payload leads\n                // back to a type this list holds.\n                if self.enums.contains(t) {\n                    return;\n                }\n                self.enums.push(t.clone());\n                for (_, p) in ty::variants(self.registry, t) {\n                    if let Some(p) = p {\n                        self.ty(&p);\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n            _ => {}\n        }\n    }\n\n    fn walk(&mut self, t: &Tir) {\n        self.ty(&t.ty);\n        match &t.kind {\n            Kind::Str(_)\n            | Kind::Int(_)\n            | Kind::Float(_)\n            | Kind::Var(_)\n            | Kind::Local(_)\n            | Kind::Input\n            | Kind::Inputs\n            | Kind::Lines\n            | Kind::Dsv { .. } => {}\n            Kind::VecLit(items) => items.iter().for_each(|i| self.walk(i)),\n            Kind::RecordLit { fields } => {\n                fields.iter().for_each(|(_, v)| self.walk(v));\n            }\n            Kind::EnumLit { payload, .. } => {\n                if let Some(p) = payload {\n                    self.walk(p);\n                }\n            }\n            Kind::Call { arg, .. } => {\n                if let Some(a) = arg {\n                    self.walk(a);\n                }\n            }\n            Kind::Concat(l, r)\n            | Kind::Compare { lhs: l, rhs: r, .. }\n            | Kind::Logic { lhs: l, rhs: r, .. }\n            | Kind::Arith { lhs: l, rhs: r, .. } => {\n                self.walk(l);\n                self.walk(r);\n            }\n            Kind::Bind { value, body, .. } => {\n                self.walk(value);\n                self.walk(body);\n            }\n            Kind::Map { source, body, .. } | Kind::OptMap { source, body, .. } => {\n                self.walk(source);\n                self.walk(body);\n            }\n            Kind::Select { source, pred, .. } => {\n                self.walk(source);\n                self.walk(pred);\n            }\n            Kind::Field { base, .. } | Kind::Unwrap { base } | Kind::Not(base) => self.walk(base),\n            Kind::Index { base, index, .. } => {\n                self.walk(base);\n                self.walk(index);\n            }\n            Kind::Slice { base, start, end, .. } => {\n                self.walk(base);\n                if let Some(s) = start {\n                    self.walk(s);\n                }\n                if let Some(e) = end {\n                    self.walk(e);\n                }\n            }\n            Kind::Match { subject, arms, .. } => {\n                self.walk(subject);\n                for a in arms {\n                    if let Some(g) = &a.guard {\n                        self.walk(g);\n                    }\n                    self.walk(&a.body);\n                }\n            }\n            Kind::Builtin { which, arg } => {\n                match which {\n                    Builtin::IntToStr => self.used.itoa = true,\n                    Builtin::JsonLines => {\n                        self.used.jsonlines = true;\n                        let elem =\n                            tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty).expect(\"checked to be a Vec or a stream\");\n                        self.used.jsonlines_has_scalar |= has_scalar(self.registry, elem);\n                    }\n                    // Purely textually gated below, like tlAt and tlRange: nothing here needs\n                    // the element type, so there is nothing to record on the walk.\n                    Builtin::IntToI64\n                    | Builtin::Range\n                    | Builtin::Collect\n                    | Builtin::Length\n                    | Builtin::Flatten\n                    | Builtin::Tail\n                    | Builtin::Fields\n                    | Builtin::Chars\n                    | Builtin::Sort\n                    | Builtin::Reverse\n                    | Builtin::Sum\n                    | Builtin::Max => {}\n                }\n                self.walk(arg);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Emitter<'a> {\n    records: Vec<Type>,\n    enums: Vec<Type>,\n    /// Every enum the program declared, for `ty::variants`: what a `Type::Enum` carries is a\n    /// placeholder wherever a recursive enum's payload reaches back to itself.\n    registry: &'a Enums,\n}\n\nimpl Emitter<'_> {\n    fn user(&self, name: &str) -> String {\n        format!(\"v_{name}\")\n    }\n\n    fn local(&self, id: LocalId) -> String {\n        format!(\"t_{id}\")\n    }\n\n    fn go_type(&self, ty: &Type) -> String {\n        match ty {\n            Type::Str => \"string\".to_string(),\n            // A sink is a joined string at runtime, so a `-> Sink` function has one here too.\n            Type::Sink => \"string\".to_string(),\n            // The default Int is 32 bits and wraps, and Go's int32 does exactly that for free.\n            Type::Int => \"int32\".to_string(),\n            // Same story a word wider (kantord/toylang#83).\n            Type::Int64 => \"int64\".to_string(),\n            Type::Bool => \"bool\".to_string(),\n            // Same width as Int: a Char is a codepoint, and the checker already refuses to mix\n            // the two.\n            Type::Char => \"int32\".to_string(),\n            Type::Float => unreachable!(\"Float is JS-only in this row\"),\n            Type::Vec(e) => format!(\"[]{}\", self.go_type(e)),\n            Type::Enum { .. } if ty.as_opt().is_some() => {\n                format!(\"tlOpt[{}]\", self.go_type(ty.as_opt().expect(\"guarded\")))\n            }\n            // Materialized eagerly as the slice of its entries, so it is a slice here too.\n            // Fusion is what will remove this materialization.\n            Type::Stream(e) => format!(\"[]{}\", self.go_type(e)),\n            Type::Record(_) => {\n                let i = self\n                    .records\n                    .iter()\n                    .position(|r| r == ty)\n                    .expect(\"every record reachable from the program was collected\");\n                format!(\"tlRec{i}\")\n            }\n            // The enum's identity -- name plus arguments -- names the struct: `ident()`\n            // embeds the arguments so each instantiation gets its own.\n            Type::Enum { .. } => format!(\"tlE_{}\", ty.ident()),\n            Type::Param(_) => unreachable!(\"params are substituted before emit\"),\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// The declaration index of `variant`, which is its tag and its pointer field's suffix.\n    /// A partial chain's yield is an Opt, so a present arm wraps its value in the tag\n    /// struct; total chains return the body bare. Split out of `expr`'s match arm to keep\n    /// the tagging decision from deepening it (kantord/toylang#62).\n    fn arm_yield(go_ty: String, body: String, partial: bool) -> String {\n        if partial {\n            format!(\"{go_ty}{{true, {body}}}\")\n        } else {\n            body\n        }\n    }\n\n    fn variant_index(variants: &[(String, Option<Type>)], variant: &str) -> usize {\n        variants\n            .iter()\n            .position(|(n, _)| n == variant)\n            .expect(\"the checker resolved the variant against this enum\")\n    }\n\n    /// The decoder for one enum: a bare string resolves among the unit variants, a single-key\n    /// object among the payload ones, and a near miss is refused naming the enum.\n    fn enum_unmarshal(&self, en: &Type, name: &str) -> String {\n        let variants = ty::variants(self.registry, en);\n        let ename = self.go_type(en);\n        let mut out = String::new();\n        out.push_str(&format!(\n            \"func (e *{ename}) UnmarshalJSON(b []byte) error {{\\n\"\n        ));\n        out.push_str(\"\\tvar s string\\n\");\n        out.push_str(\"\\tif json.Unmarshal(b, &s) == nil {\\n\");\n        out.push_str(\"\\t\\tswitch s {\\n\");\n        for (i, (vname, payload)) in variants.iter().enumerate() {\n            if payload.is_none() {\n                out.push_str(&format!(\n                    \"\\t\\tcase \\\"{vname}\\\":\\n\\t\\t\\t*e = {ename}{{tag: {i}}}\\n\\t\\t\\treturn nil\\n\"\n                ));\n            }\n        }\n        out.push_str(\"\\t\\t}\\n\");\n        out.push_str(&format!(\n            \"\\t\\treturn fmt.Errorf(\\\"`%s` is not a unit variant of {name}\\\", s)\\n\\t}}\\n\"\n        ));\n        let payloads: Vec<(usize, &String, &Type)> = variants\n            .iter()\n            .enumerate()\n            .filter_map(|(i, (v, p))| p.as_ref().map(|p| (i, v, p)))\n            .collect();\n        if payloads.is_empty() {\n            out.push_str(&format!(\"\\treturn fmt.Errorf(\\\"expected {name}\\\")\\n}}\\n\\n\"));\n            return out;\n        }\n        out.push_str(\"\\tvar m map[string]json.RawMessage\\n\");\n        out.push_str(\"\\tif err := json.Unmarshal(b, &m); err != nil || len(m) != 1 {\\n\");\n        out.push_str(&format!(\n            \"\\t\\treturn fmt.Errorf(\\\"expected {name}\\\")\\n\\t}}\\n\"\n        ));\n        out.push_str(\"\\tfor k, v := range m {\\n\");\n        out.push_str(\"\\t\\tswitch k {\\n\");\n        for (i, vname, pty) in &payloads {\n            out.push_str(&format!(\"\\t\\tcase \\\"{vname}\\\":\\n\"));\n            out.push_str(&format!(\"\\t\\t\\tvar p {}\\n\", self.go_type(pty)));\n            out.push_str(\"\\t\\t\\tif err := json.Unmarshal(v, &p); err != nil {\\n\\t\\t\\t\\treturn err\\n\\t\\t\\t}\\n\");\n            out.push_str(&format!(\n                \"\\t\\t\\t*e = {ename}{{tag: {i}, p{i}: &p}}\\n\\t\\t\\treturn nil\\n\"\n            ));\n        }\n        out.push_str(\"\\t\\t}\\n\");\n        out.push_str(&format!(\n            \"\\t\\treturn fmt.Errorf(\\\"`%s` is not a payload variant of {name}\\\", k)\\n\\t}}\\n\"\n        ));\n        out.push_str(&format!(\"\\treturn fmt.Errorf(\\\"expected {name}\\\")\\n}}\\n\\n\"));\n        out\n    }\n\n    /// A `jsonlines(f(inputs))` program, compiled as a loop that decodes one JSON value at a\n    /// time (the same `json.Decoder`-until-`io.EOF` loop the eager `inputs` path already used,\n    /// just without collecting into a slice first) and prints it immediately. No explicit flush\n    /// is needed: unlike Rust, Python, jq and Lua, `fmt.Println` writes straight through to the\n    /// underlying `os.Stdout` file descriptor with no userspace buffering in between.\n    ///\n    /// Each stage's binding is followed by `_ = t_N`: it is a `:=` local, not a closure parameter\n    /// the way the eager path's `tlMap`/`tlSelect` callback argument is, and Go rejects an unused\n    /// local at compile time -- a `select` whose predicate is the only use, or a `map` whose body\n    /// ignores its argument entirely, would otherwise fail to build only in the fused case.\n    fn fused_main(&self, program: &Program, fusion: &tir::Fusion) -> String {\n        let mut out = String::new();\n        out.push_str(\"func main() {\\n\");\n        let (mut current, mut current_ty) = match fusion.source {\n            tir::Source::Inputs => {\n                let elem = program\n                    .inputs\n                    .as_ref()\n                    .expect(\"an inputs source recorded its element\");\n                out.push_str(\"\\tdec := json.NewDecoder(os.Stdin)\\n\");\n                out.push_str(\"\\tfor {\\n\");\n                out.push_str(&format!(\"\\t\\tvar t_line {}\\n\", self.go_type(elem)));\n                out.push_str(\n                    \"\\t\\tif err := dec.Decode(&t_line); err != nil {\\n\\t\\t\\tif err == io.EOF {\\n\\t\\t\\t\\tbreak\\n\\t\\t\\t}\\n\\t\\t\\ttlFail(err.Error())\\n\\t\\t}\\n\",\n                );\n                (\"t_line\".to_string(), elem.clone())\n            }\n            // The same scanner the eager collect helper uses, one line per Scan; a raw line is\n            // already the element, blank ones included.\n            tir::Source::Lines => {\n                out.push_str(\"\\ts := bufio.NewScanner(os.Stdin)\\n\");\n                out.push_str(\"\\ts.Buffer(make([]byte, 0, 65536), 1024*1024)\\n\");\n                out.push_str(\"\\ts.Split(tlScanLines)\\n\");\n                out.push_str(\"\\tfor s.Scan() {\\n\");\n                out.push_str(\"\\t\\tt_line := s.Text()\\n\");\n                (\"t_line\".to_string(), Type::Str)\n            }\n            // The bound is evaluated once; the loop counter is the element. A negative bound\n            // clamps to zero, the same answer `tlRange` gives eagerly.\n            tir::Source::Range(bound) => {\n                out.push_str(&format!(\"\\tn := {}\\n\", self.expr(bound)));\n                out.push_str(\"\\tif n < 0 {\\n\\t\\tn = 0\\n\\t}\\n\");\n                out.push_str(\"\\tfor t_i := int32(0); t_i < n; t_i++ {\\n\");\n                (\"t_i\".to_string(), Type::Int)\n            }\n        };\n        for stage in &fusion.stages {\n            match stage {\n                tir::Stage::Map { param, body } => {\n                    out.push_str(&format!(\n                        \"\\t\\t{} := {}\\n\\t\\t_ = {}\\n\",\n                        self.local(*param),\n                        current,\n                        self.local(*param)\n                    ));\n                    current = self.expr(body);\n                    current_ty = body.ty.clone();\n                }\n                tir::Stage::Select { param, pred } => {\n                    out.push_str(&format!(\n                        \"\\t\\t{} := {}\\n\\t\\t_ = {}\\n\",\n                        self.local(*param),\n                        current,\n                        self.local(*param)\n                    ));\n                    out.push_str(&format!(\n                        \"\\t\\tif !({}) {{\\n\\t\\t\\tcontinue\\n\\t\\t}}\\n\",\n                        self.expr(pred)\n                    ));\n                    current = self.local(*param);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        let printed = self.show(&current_ty, &current, 0);\n        out.push_str(&format!(\"\\t\\tfmt.Println({printed})\\n\"));\n        out.push_str(\"\\t}\\n}\\n\");\n        out\n    }\n\n    /// Wrap `leaf` in `depth` layers of `tlMap`, spelling the element type at each one.\n    ///\n    /// This is what the other backends get for free from a `depth` argument. Here the map at\n    /// each level is a different instantiation, so the types have to be written down -- and they\n    /// can be, because every node in the IR carries the type it was checked at.\n    fn distribute(\n        &self,\n        value: &str,\n        value_ty: &Type,\n        result_ty: &Type,\n        depth: usize,\n        leaf: &dyn Fn(&str) -> String,\n    ) -> String {\n        if depth == 0 {\n            return leaf(value);\n        }\n        let elem = tir::runtime_elem(value_ty).expect(\"a dimension to distribute over\");\n        let result_elem = tir::runtime_elem(result_ty).expect(\"the result keeps the dimension\");\n        let var = format!(\"m{depth}\");\n        format!(\n            \"tlMap({value}, func({var} {}) {} {{ return {} }})\",\n            self.go_type(elem),\n            self.go_type(result_elem),\n            self.distribute(&var, elem, result_elem, depth - 1, leaf)\n        )\n    }\n\n    fn expr(&self, t: &Tir) -> String {\n        match &t.kind {\n            Kind::Str(s) => go_string(s),\n            Kind::Int(n) => int_lit(&t.ty, *n),\n            Kind::Float(_) => unreachable!(\"Float is JS-only in this row\"),\n            Kind::Var(name) => self.user(name),\n            Kind::Local(id) => self.local(*id),\n            Kind::Input => INPUT.to_string(),\n            Kind::Inputs => INPUTS.to_string(),\n            // The stream, materialized eagerly: whatever consumes it -- `collect`, a mapper --\n            // works on the slice of its entries.\n            Kind::Lines => \"tlCollectLines()\".to_string(),\n            Kind::Dsv { delim } => format!(\n                \"tlDsv(tlCollectLines(), {})\",\n                go_string(delim)\n            ),\n            // go_type resolves the struct name, and the collector registered it because a\n            // record literal carries its own record type.\n            Kind::RecordLit { fields } => {\n                let parts: Vec<String> = fields\n                    .iter()\n                    .map(|(name, value)| format!(\"F{name}: {}\", self.expr(value)))\n                    .collect();\n                format!(\"{}{{{}}}\", self.go_type(&t.ty), parts.join(\", \"))\n            }\n\n            Kind::EnumLit { variant, payload } => {\n                // Opt's constructors build its tlOpt encoding, not a tag struct: `some(x)`\n                // is presence, `none` is the zero value.\n                if t.ty.as_opt().is_some() {\n                    return match payload {\n                        Some(p) => {\n                            format!(\"{}{{true, {}}}\", self.go_type(&t.ty), self.expr(p))\n                        }\n                        None => format!(\"{}{{}}\", self.go_type(&t.ty)),\n                    };\n                }\n                let i = Self::variant_index(&ty::variants(self.registry, &t.ty), variant);\n                match payload {\n                    None => format!(\"{}{{tag: {i}}}\", self.go_type(&t.ty)),\n                    Some(p) => format!(\n                        \"{}{{tag: {i}, p{i}: tlPtr({})}}\",\n                        self.go_type(&t.ty),\n                        self.expr(p)\n                    ),\n                }\n            }\n\n            Kind::VecLit(items) => {\n                let parts: Vec<String> = items.iter().map(|i| self.expr(i)).collect();\n                format!(\"{}{{{}}}\", self.go_type(&t.ty), parts.join(\", \"))\n            }\n            Kind::Call { func, arg } => format!(\n                \"{}({})\",\n                self.user(func),\n                arg.as_deref().map_or_else(String::new, |a| self.expr(a))\n            ),\n            Kind::Concat(l, r) => self.concat(&t.ty, l, r),\n            Kind::Arith { op, lhs, rhs } => arith(&t.ty, *op, self.expr(lhs), self.expr(rhs)),\n            // Go's own `&&`/`||`, which short-circuit, so the right side stays unevaluated\n            // exactly where toylang says it does.\n            Kind::Logic { op, lhs, rhs } => {\n                let op = match op {\n                    LogicOp::And => \"&&\",\n                    LogicOp::Or => \"||\",\n                };\n                format!(\"({} {op} {})\", self.expr(lhs), self.expr(rhs))\n            }\n            Kind::Not(base) => format!(\"(!{})\", self.expr(base)),\n            Kind::Builtin { which, arg } => match which {\n                Builtin::IntToStr => format!(\"strconv.FormatInt(int64({}), 10)\", self.expr(arg)),\n                Builtin::IntToI64 => format!(\"int64({})\", self.expr(arg)),\n                Builtin::Range => format!(\"tlRange({})\", self.expr(arg)),\n                Builtin::Chars => format!(\"tlChars({})\", self.expr(arg)),\n                Builtin::JsonLines => {\n                    let elem = tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty).expect(\"checked to be a Vec or a stream\");\n                    let e = \"e0\".to_string();\n                    format!(\n                        \"tlJsonlines({}, func({e} {}) string {{ return {} }})\",\n                        self.expr(arg),\n                        self.go_type(elem),\n                        self.show(elem, &e, 1)\n                    )\n                }\n                // The source already materialized, so the exit has nothing left to do.\n                Builtin::Collect => self.expr(arg),\n                Builtin::Length => format!(\"int32(len({}))\", self.expr(arg)),\n                Builtin::Tail => format!(\"tlTail({})\", self.expr(arg)),\n                Builtin::Flatten => format!(\"tlFlatten({})\", self.expr(arg)),\n                Builtin::Sort => format!(\"tlSort({})\", self.expr(arg)),\n                Builtin::Reverse => format!(\"tlReverse({})\", self.expr(arg)),\n                // Which fold depends on the element width: int32 and int64 are distinct types in\n                // Go, so they cannot share one helper the way a dynamically typed backend can.\n                Builtin::Sum => {\n                    if tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty) == Some(&Type::Int) {\n                        format!(\"tlSum({})\", self.expr(arg))\n                    } else {\n                        format!(\"tlSum64({})\", self.expr(arg))\n                    }\n                }\n                Builtin::Max => format!(\"tlMax({})\", self.expr(arg)),\n                // The names come from the checked type, not the struct value, so `arg` runs in\n                // an ignored parameter -- the same IIFE shape `Bind` uses -- purely for whatever\n                // else it does.\n                Builtin::Fields => {\n                    let Type::Record(fields) = &arg.ty else {\n                        unreachable!(\"checked to be a record\")\n                    };\n                    let names: Vec<String> = fields.iter().map(|(n, _)| go_string(n)).collect();\n                    format!(\n                        \"func(_ {}) []string {{ return []string{{{}}} }}({})\",\n                        self.go_type(&arg.ty),\n                        names.join(\", \"),\n                        self.expr(arg)\n                    )\n                }\n            },\n            Kind::Compare { op, lhs, rhs } => self.compare(*op, lhs, rhs),\n            Kind::Bind {\n                local: id,\n                value,\n                body,\n            } => format!(\n                \"func({} {}) {} {{ return {} }}({})\",\n                self.local(*id),\n                self.go_type(&value.ty),\n                self.go_type(&t.ty),\n                self.expr(body),\n                self.expr(value)\n            ),\n            Kind::Map {\n                source,\n                param,\n                body,\n            } => format!(\n                \"tlMap({}, func({} {}) {} {{ return {} }})\",\n                self.expr(source),\n                self.local(*param),\n                self.go_type(tir::runtime_elem(&source.ty).expect(\"map runs over a dimension\")),\n                self.go_type(&body.ty),\n                self.expr(body)\n            ),\n            Kind::Select {\n                source,\n                param,\n                pred,\n            } => format!(\n                \"tlSelect({}, func({} {}) bool {{ return {} }})\",\n                self.expr(source),\n                self.local(*param),\n                self.go_type(tir::runtime_elem(&source.ty).expect(\"select runs over a dimension\")),\n                self.expr(pred)\n            ),\n            // Opt's reorder pass (kantord/toylang#66): the same `!o.ok`/`.v` shape tlUnwrap and\n            // the printer already branch on, generalised to rebuild the tlOpt instead of\n            // reading through it. `__srcOpt` binds the source once, so evaluating it twice (the\n            // `.ok` test, then `.v`) costs nothing beyond a field read.\n            Kind::OptMap {\n                source,\n                param,\n                body,\n            } => format!(\n                \"func(__srcOpt {}) {} {{ if !__srcOpt.ok {{ return {1}{{}} }}; {} := __srcOpt.v; _ = {2}; return {1}{{true, {}}} }}({})\",\n                self.go_type(&source.ty),\n                self.go_type(&t.ty),\n                self.local(*param),\n                self.expr(body),\n                self.expr(source)\n            ),\n            Kind::Field { base, name } => {\n                let depth = tir::vec_depth(&base.ty);\n                self.distribute(&self.expr(base), &base.ty, &t.ty, depth, &|v| {\n                    format!(\"({v}).F{name}\")\n                })\n            }\n            Kind::Unwrap { base } => {\n                let depth = tir::vec_depth(&base.ty);\n                self.distribute(&self.expr(base), &base.ty, &t.ty, depth, &|v| {\n                    format!(\"tlUnwrap({v})\")\n                })\n            }\n            Kind::Index {\n                base, index, depth, ..\n            } => {\n                let i = self.expr(index);\n                self.distribute(&self.expr(base), &base.ty, &t.ty, *depth, &|v| {\n                    format!(\"tlAt({v}, {i})\")\n                })\n            }\n            Kind::Slice {\n                base, start, end, depth,\n            } => {\n                let lo = match start {\n                    Some(s) => self.expr(s),\n                    None => \"-2147483648\".to_string(),\n                };\n                let hi = match end {\n                    Some(e) => self.expr(e),\n                    None => \"2147483647\".to_string(),\n                };\n                self.distribute(&self.expr(base), &base.ty, &t.ty, *depth, &|v| {\n                    format!(\"tlSlice({v}, {lo}, {hi})\")\n                })\n            }\n            // Tests over the subject (a plain local, so re-reading it is free): a tag test for\n            // a variant arm, the same chain the enum printer uses, and the guard's own Bool\n            // for a guard arm. A total chain's last arm needs no test, the checker having\n            // proved nothing else can reach it; a partial chain tests every arm, wraps each\n            // body in the present Opt, and falls through to the absent one. `_ =` after a\n            // payload binding, as in the fused loop, because Go rejects an unused local and a\n            // body is free to ignore its payload.\n            Kind::Match {\n                subject,\n                arms,\n                partial,\n            } => {\n                let subj = self.expr(subject);\n                let mut body = String::new();\n                for (i, arm) in arms.iter().enumerate() {\n                    let mut run = String::new();\n                    if let Some(pid) = arm.payload {\n                        let variant = arm\n                            .variant\n                            .as_ref()\n                            .expect(\"only a variant arm has a payload\");\n                        let vi =\n                            Self::variant_index(&ty::variants(self.registry, &subject.ty), variant);\n                        run.push_str(&format!(\n                            \"{} := *{subj}.p{vi}; _ = {}; \",\n                            self.local(pid),\n                            self.local(pid)\n                        ));\n                    }\n                    let produced =\n                        Self::arm_yield(self.go_type(&t.ty), self.expr(&arm.body), *partial);\n                    run.push_str(&format!(\"return {produced}\"));\n                    let test = match (&arm.variant, &arm.guard) {\n                        (Some(v), _) => Some(format!(\n                            \"{subj}.tag == {}\",\n                            Self::variant_index(&ty::variants(self.registry, &subject.ty), v)\n                        )),\n                        (None, Some(g)) => Some(self.expr(g)),\n                        (None, None) => None,\n                    };\n                    match test {\n                        Some(test) if *partial || i + 1 < arms.len() => {\n                            body.push_str(&format!(\"if {test} {{ {run} }}; \"));\n                        }\n                        _ => body.push_str(&run),\n                    }\n                }\n                if *partial {\n                    body.push_str(&format!(\"return {}{{}}\", self.go_type(&t.ty)));\n                }\n                format!(\"func() {} {{ {body} }}()\", self.go_type(&t.ty))\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Go has no `+` for slices, so a Vec joins through the same helper `flatten` uses, wrapped\n    /// around a two-entry outer literal rather than through a second helper.\n    fn concat(&self, ty: &Type, l: &Tir, r: &Tir) -> String {\n        match ty {\n            Type::Vec(_) => {\n                let go_ty = self.go_type(ty);\n                let (l, r) = (self.expr(l), self.expr(r));\n                format!(\"tlFlatten([]{go_ty}{{{l}, {r}}})\")\n            }\n            _ => format!(\"({} + {})\", self.expr(l), self.expr(r)),\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// The printer is built from the type rather than by inspecting the value, as on every other\n    /// backend. Here there is no choice at all: a Go value cannot be asked what it is.\n    /// Equality on a composite is structural, which Go's own `==` is not: it compares an enum's\n    /// payload pointer by address (kantord/toylang#68). Ordering on a composite is a separate\n    /// open question and keeps whatever Go does with it.\n    fn compare(&self, op: BinOp, lhs: &Tir, rhs: &Tir) -> String {\n        if !lhs.ty.is_composite() || !matches!(op, BinOp::Eq | BinOp::Ne) {\n            return format!(\"({} {} {})\", self.expr(lhs), go_op(op), self.expr(rhs));\n        }\n        let call = format!(\"tlEq({}, {})\", self.expr(lhs), self.expr(rhs));\n        match op {\n            BinOp::Ne => format!(\"(!{call})\"),\n            _ => call,\n        }\n    }\n\n    fn show(&self, ty: &Type, value: &str, depth: usize) -> String {\n        match ty {\n            Type::Param(_) => unreachable!(\"params are substituted before emit\"),\n            // The checker refuses a program whose result contains a stream, since there is\n            // nothing to print: a stream has no value, only a promise that collect can redeem.\n            Type::Stream(_) => unreachable!(\"a stream cannot reach the printer\"),\n            Type::Char => unreachable!(\"Char cannot reach the printer, refused by the checker\"),\n            Type::Str => format!(\"tlQuote({value})\"),\n            Type::Sink => unreachable!(\"a sink only ever prints raw, never through the printer\"),\n            Type::Int => format!(\"strconv.FormatInt(int64({value}), 10)\"),\n            Type::Int64 => format!(\"strconv.FormatInt({value}, 10)\"),\n            Type::Float => unreachable!(\"Float is JS-only in this row\"),","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":1320,"totalLines":1321,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/emit_go.rs","time":{"start":1788373519433,"end":1788373519455}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Good. The core Float infra is already in the worktree (parse, check, ty, tir, tags, float.rs, input.rs). The Go backend currently has `unreachable!(\"Float is JS-only in this row\")` in three places:\n- src/emit_go.rs:818 (go_type)\n- src/emit_go.rs:1025 (expr / Kind::Float)\n- src/emit_go.rs:1320 (show/printer Type::Float)\n\nAlso `has_scalar` returns false for Float (fine - it means no strconv import needed since Float prints as a plain number).\n\nNow I need to implement Float for the Go backend. The reference is the JS implementation in emit_js.rs. Let me read emit_go.rs fully to understand its structure, then look at how JS implements Float and translate it to Go.\n\nLet me read emit_go.rs.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0635e4b02001KR5M625z0NlZAO","sessionID":"ses_f9ca2427bffeVwNvwc6e496rx3","messageID":"msg_0635e3333001rPdYW1mD71IRSw"}}
