{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788373628237,"sessionID":"ses_f9ca2427bffeVwNvwc6e496rx3","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b03880a4623e663c","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"git show f22a806 -- tests/backend_js.rs build.rs src/input.rs src/lib.rs src/tags.rs"},"output":"commit f22a8060efeb4cc81b8722763ee401b0943bca2e\nAuthor: Daniel Kantor <git@daniel-kantor.com>\nDate:   Wed Sep 2 10:08:40 2026 +0200\n\n    Add Float for the JS backend (IEEE binary64, ADR 0007)\n    \n    A decimal-point literal is a Float: a separate type from Int with no\n    implicit conversion, so mixing the two in one operator is an error the\n    same way mixing Int and Int64 is. Division is total -- a zero divisor\n    returns Infinity, the IEEE answer, where Int stops the program -- and\n    NaN and Infinity print as their names. A Float reads off the wire as\n    the JSON number it already is, the case that made Int64 refuse input.\n    JS emits plain number operators and prints through String().\n    \n    The other six backends get unreachable arms for Float nodes and types:\n    their exhaustive matches over the shared Type/Kind enums have to\n    compile, and Float never reaches them while it is JS-only. Their real\n    implementations are follow-up rows, not this one.\n    \n    Derived: binary64 representation and total division (ADR 0007, the Q37\n    ruling), Float-in-input reading a JSON number, the separate-type and\n    no-implicit-conversion rules (Int64's precedent)\n    Agent-invented: float literal grammar (. and exponent only join when\n    digits follow), the shortest-round-trip literal spelling in src/float.rs\n    Written by DeepSeek V4 Flash via opencode.\n    \n    Co-Authored-By: DeepSeek V4 Flash <deepseek-v4-flash>\n\ndiff --git a/build.rs b/build.rs\nindex 05fa5b1..2c77f5a 100644\n--- a/build.rs\n+++ b/build.rs\n@@ -68,6 +68,12 @@ impl ToRust for i64 {\n     }\n }\n \n+impl ToRust for f64 {\n+    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n+        format!(\"{self}f64\")\n+    }\n+}\n+\n impl ToRust for u32 {\n     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n         format!(\"{self}u32\")\n@@ -143,6 +149,7 @@ impl ToRust for ty::Type {\n             ty::Type::Str => \"crate::ty::Type::Str\".to_string(),\n             ty::Type::Int => \"crate::ty::Type::Int\".to_string(),\n             ty::Type::Int64 => \"crate::ty::Type::Int64\".to_string(),\n+            ty::Type::Float => \"crate::ty::Type::Float\".to_string(),\n             ty::Type::Bool => \"crate::ty::Type::Bool\".to_string(),\n             ty::Type::Char => \"crate::ty::Type::Char\".to_string(),\n             ty::Type::Vec(elem) => format!(\"crate::ty::Type::Vec({})\", elem.to_rust()),\n@@ -360,6 +367,7 @@ impl ToRust for tir::Kind {\n         match self {\n             Str(s) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Str({})\", s.to_rust()),\n             Int(n) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Int({})\", n.to_rust()),\n+            Float(n) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Float({})\", n.to_rust()),\n             VecLit(items) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::VecLit({})\", items.to_rust()),\n             RecordLit { fields } => record_lit(fields),\n             EnumLit { variant, payload } => enum_lit(variant, payload),\ndiff --git a/src/input.rs b/src/input.rs\nindex 3f4a6d9..6134c1b 100644\n--- a/src/input.rs\n+++ b/src/input.rs\n@@ -30,6 +30,12 @@ pub fn validate(enums: &Enums, value: &Value, ty: &Type, path: &str) -> Result<(\n             };\n         }\n \n+        (Type::Float, Value::Number(_)) => {\n+            // A Float is the double a JSON number already is (ADR 0007), so every JSON number\n+            // -- integer or not -- reads as one, with no exactness to lose the way Int64 does.\n+            return Ok(());\n+        }\n+\n         (Type::Vec(elem), Value::Array(items)) => {\n             for (i, item) in items.iter().enumerate() {\n                 validate(enums, item, elem, &format!(\"{path}[{i}]\"))?;\ndiff --git a/src/lib.rs b/src/lib.rs\nindex b0f20d1..6eda96b 100644\n--- a/src/lib.rs\n+++ b/src/lib.rs\n@@ -9,6 +9,7 @@ pub mod emit_py;\n pub mod emit_rs;\n pub mod emit_toylang;\n pub mod error;\n+pub mod float;\n pub mod fmt_tree;\n pub mod input;\n pub mod parse;\ndiff --git a/src/tags.rs b/src/tags.rs\nindex 3364201..715ed05 100644\n--- a/src/tags.rs\n+++ b/src/tags.rs\n@@ -32,6 +32,7 @@ fn walk(tir: &Tir, tags: &mut BTreeSet<String>) {\n     match &tir.kind {\n         Kind::Str(_)\n         | Kind::Int(_)\n+        | Kind::Float(_)\n         | Kind::Var(_)\n         | Kind::Local(_)\n         | Kind::Input\n@@ -111,6 +112,7 @@ fn tag(tir: &Tir) -> String {\n         // say (kantord/toylang#83), so the tag reads it rather than the kind.\n         Kind::Int(_) if tir.ty == crate::ty::Type::Int64 => \"int64\".into(),\n         Kind::Int(_) => \"int\".into(),\n+        Kind::Float(_) => \"float\".into(),\n         Kind::VecLit(_) => \"vec-literal\".into(),\n         Kind::RecordLit { .. } => \"record-literal\".into(),\n         // CONTEXT.md's terms: a `variant` is one alternative, a `unit variant` carries nothing.\ndiff --git a/tests/backend_js.rs b/tests/backend_js.rs\nindex 4f53f33..aa209f2 100644\n--- a/tests/backend_js.rs\n+++ b/tests/backend_js.rs\n@@ -31,3 +31,47 @@ first(input)\n     assert_eq!(declared_order, reversed);\n     insta::assert_snapshot!(declared_order);\n }\n+\n+// Float is JS-only in this row (kantord/toylang#149): the other backends do not carry it yet,\n+// so none of these can be a corpus case, which would require every backend to agree. They live\n+// here, JS-only, until the follow-up rows bring Float to the rest of the backends.\n+\n+/// A decimal-point literal parses, type-checks, and prints through the JS printer.\n+#[test]\n+fn float_literals_parse_and_print() {\n+    let out = toylang::run_on(\"1.5 + 0.25 * 2.0\\n\", None, Backend::Js).unwrap();\n+    assert_eq!(out, \"2\\n\");\n+}\n+\n+/// Division on a Float is total (ADR 0007, the Q37 ruling): a zero divisor is the IEEE answer,\n+/// Infinity, not the stop an Int's `1 / 0` is.\n+#[test]\n+fn float_division_by_zero_is_infinity() {\n+    let out = toylang::run_on(\"1.0 / 0.0\\n\", None, Backend::Js).unwrap();\n+    assert_eq!(out, \"Infinity\\n\");\n+}\n+\n+/// NaN and Infinity are values a Float can hold, and both print as their names.\n+#[test]\n+fn float_nan_and_infinity_are_producible() {\n+    let nan = toylang::run_on(\"0.0 / 0.0\\n\", None, Backend::Js).unwrap();\n+    assert_eq!(nan, \"NaN\\n\");\n+    let neg = toylang::run_on(\"-1.0 / 0.0\\n\", None, Backend::Js).unwrap();\n+    assert_eq!(neg, \"-Infinity\\n\");\n+}\n+\n+/// Float comparisons type-check and print a Bool.\n+#[test]\n+fn float_comparison() {\n+    let out = toylang::run_on(\"1.5 < 2.5\\n\", None, Backend::Js).unwrap();\n+    assert_eq!(out, \"true\\n\");\n+}\n+\n+/// A Float result keeps its value when read back off the wire: a JSON number already is the\n+/// double a Float names (ADR 0007), the case that made Int64 refuse `input`.\n+#[test]\n+fn float_input_reads_a_json_number() {\n+    let src = \"fn twice(x: Float) -> Float = x * 2.0\\n\\ntwice(input)\\n\";\n+    let out = toylang::run_on(src, Some(\"2.5\"), Backend::Js).unwrap();\n+    assert_eq!(out, \"5\\n\");\n+}\n","metadata":{"output":"commit f22a8060efeb4cc81b8722763ee401b0943bca2e\nAuthor: Daniel Kantor <git@daniel-kantor.com>\nDate:   Wed Sep 2 10:08:40 2026 +0200\n\n    Add Float for the JS backend (IEEE binary64, ADR 0007)\n    \n    A decimal-point literal is a Float: a separate type from Int with no\n    implicit conversion, so mixing the two in one operator is an error the\n    same way mixing Int and Int64 is. Division is total -- a zero divisor\n    returns Infinity, the IEEE answer, where Int stops the program -- and\n    NaN and Infinity print as their names. A Float reads off the wire as\n    the JSON number it already is, the case that made Int64 refuse input.\n    JS emits plain number operators and prints through String().\n    \n    The other six backends get unreachable arms for Float nodes and types:\n    their exhaustive matches over the shared Type/Kind enums have to\n    compile, and Float never reaches them while it is JS-only. Their real\n    implementations are follow-up rows, not this one.\n    \n    Derived: binary64 representation and total division (ADR 0007, the Q37\n    ruling), Float-in-input reading a JSON number, the separate-type and\n    no-implicit-conversion rules (Int64's precedent)\n    Agent-invented: float literal grammar (. and exponent only join when\n    digits follow), the shortest-round-trip literal spelling in src/float.rs\n    Written by DeepSeek V4 Flash via opencode.\n    \n    Co-Authored-By: DeepSeek V4 Flash <deepseek-v4-flash>\n\ndiff --git a/build.rs b/build.rs\nindex 05fa5b1..2c77f5a 100644\n--- a/build.rs\n+++ b/build.rs\n@@ -68,6 +68,12 @@ impl ToRust for i64 {\n     }\n }\n \n+impl ToRust for f64 {\n+    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n+        format!(\"{self}f64\")\n+    }\n+}\n+\n impl ToRust for u32 {\n     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n         format!(\"{self}u32\")\n@@ -143,6 +149,7 @@ impl ToRust for ty::Type {\n             ty::Type::Str => \"crate::ty::Type::Str\".to_string(),\n             ty::Type::Int => \"crate::ty::Type::Int\".to_string(),\n             ty::Type::Int64 => \"crate::ty::Type::Int64\".to_string(),\n+            ty::Type::Float => \"crate::ty::Type::Float\".to_string(),\n             ty::Type::Bool => \"crate::ty::Type::Bool\".to_string(),\n             ty::Type::Char => \"crate::ty::Type::Char\".to_string(),\n             ty::Type::Vec(elem) => format!(\"crate::ty::Type::Vec({})\", elem.to_rust()),\n@@ -360,6 +367,7 @@ impl ToRust for tir::Kind {\n         match self {\n             Str(s) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Str({})\", s.to_rust()),\n             Int(n) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Int({})\", n.to_rust()),\n+            Float(n) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Float({})\", n.to_rust()),\n             VecLit(items) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::VecLit({})\", items.to_rust()),\n             RecordLit { fields } => record_lit(fields),\n             EnumLit { variant, payload } => enum_lit(variant, payload),\ndiff --git a/src/input.rs b/src/input.rs\nindex 3f4a6d9..6134c1b 100644\n--- a/src/input.rs\n+++ b/src/input.rs\n@@ -30,6 +30,12 @@ pub fn validate(enums: &Enums, value: &Value, ty: &Type, path: &str) -> Result<(\n             };\n         }\n \n+        (Type::Float, Value::Number(_)) => {\n+            // A Float is the double a JSON number already is (ADR 0007), so every JSON number\n+            // -- integer or not -- reads as one, with no exactness to lose the way Int64 does.\n+            return Ok(());\n+        }\n+\n         (Type::Vec(elem), Value::Array(items)) => {\n             for (i, item) in items.iter().enumerate() {\n                 validate(enums, item, elem, &format!(\"{path}[{i}]\"))?;\ndiff --git a/src/lib.rs b/src/lib.rs\nindex b0f20d1..6eda96b 100644\n--- a/src/lib.rs\n+++ b/src/lib.rs\n@@ -9,6 +9,7 @@ pub mod emit_py;\n pub mod emit_rs;\n pub mod emit_toylang;\n pub mod error;\n+pub mod float;\n pub mod fmt_tree;\n pub mod input;\n pub mod parse;\ndiff --git a/src/tags.rs b/src/tags.rs\nindex 3364201..715ed05 100644\n--- a/src/tags.rs\n+++ b/src/tags.rs\n@@ -32,6 +32,7 @@ fn walk(tir: &Tir, tags: &mut BTreeSet<String>) {\n     match &tir.kind {\n         Kind::Str(_)\n         | Kind::Int(_)\n+        | Kind::Float(_)\n         | Kind::Var(_)\n         | Kind::Local(_)\n         | Kind::Input\n@@ -111,6 +112,7 @@ fn tag(tir: &Tir) -> String {\n         // say (kantord/toylang#83), so the tag reads it rather than the kind.\n         Kind::Int(_) if tir.ty == crate::ty::Type::Int64 => \"int64\".into(),\n         Kind::Int(_) => \"int\".into(),\n+        Kind::Float(_) => \"float\".into(),\n         Kind::VecLit(_) => \"vec-literal\".into(),\n         Kind::RecordLit { .. } => \"record-literal\".into(),\n         // CONTEXT.md's terms: a `variant` is one alternative, a `unit variant` carries nothing.\ndiff --git a/tests/backend_js.rs b/tests/backend_js.rs\nindex 4f53f33..aa209f2 100644\n--- a/tests/backend_js.rs\n+++ b/tests/backend_js.rs\n@@ -31,3 +31,47 @@ first(input)\n     assert_eq!(declared_order, reversed);\n     insta::assert_snapshot!(declared_order);\n }\n+\n+// Float is JS-only in this row (kantord/toylang#149): the other backends do not carry it yet,\n+// so none of these can be a corpus case, which would require every backend to agree. They live\n+// here, JS-only, until the follow-up rows bring Float to the rest of the backends.\n+\n+/// A decimal-point literal parses, type-checks, and prints through the JS printer.\n+#[test]\n+fn float_literals_parse_and_print() {\n+    let out = toylang::run_on(\"1.5 + 0.25 * 2.0\\n\", None, Backend::Js).unwrap();\n+    assert_eq!(out, \"2\\n\");\n+}\n+\n+/// Division on a Float is total (ADR 0007, the Q37 ruling): a zero divisor is the IEEE answer,\n+/// Infinity, not the stop an Int's `1 / 0` is.\n+#[test]\n+fn float_division_by_zero_is_infinity() {\n+    let out = toylang::run_on(\"1.0 / 0.0\\n\", None, Backend::Js).unwrap();\n+    assert_eq!(out, \"Infinity\\n\");\n+}\n+\n+/// NaN and Infinity are values a Float can hold, and both print as their names.\n+#[test]\n+fn float_nan_and_infinity_are_producible() {\n+    let nan = toylang::run_on(\"0.0 / 0.0\\n\", None, Backend::Js).unwrap();\n+    assert_eq!(nan, \"NaN\\n\");\n+    let neg = toylang::run_on(\"-1.0 / 0.0\\n\", None, Backend::Js).unwrap();\n+    assert_eq!(neg, \"-Infinity\\n\");\n+}\n+\n+/// Float comparisons type-check and print a Bool.\n+#[test]\n+fn float_comparison() {\n+    let out = toylang::run_on(\"1.5 < 2.5\\n\", None, Backend::Js).unwrap();\n+    assert_eq!(out, \"true\\n\");\n+}\n+\n+/// A Float result keeps its value when read back off the wire: a JSON number already is the\n+/// double a Float names (ADR 0007), the case that made Int64 refuse `input`.\n+#[test]\n+fn float_input_reads_a_json_number() {\n+    let src = \"fn twice(x: Float) -> Float = x * 2.0\\n\\ntwice(input)\\n\";\n+    let out = toylang::run_on(src, Some(\"2.5\"), Backend::Js).unwrap();\n+    assert_eq!(out, \"5\\n\");\n+}\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"git show f22a806 -- tests/backend_js.rs build.rs src/input.rs src/lib.rs src/tags.rs","time":{"start":1788373628177,"end":1788373628180}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I understand the Go emitter fully. Let me look at how the JS backend implemented things, and how the Python/Rust sibling implementations look (they may already be committed in this branch? Let me check the current branch state — are the float-build-rust/python lanes merged yet?).\n\nLet me check whether emit_rs.rs / emit_py.rs already have Float implemented (in case a sibling landed and I can copy the approach). The grep above showed emit_rs.rs has `Kind::Float(_) => unreachable!(\"Float is JS-only in this row\")` and emit_py.rs too. So those lanes haven't landed yet.\n\nNow, my task: implement Float for the Go backend. Key pieces:\n\n1. **`go_type`**: `Type::Float => \"float64\"` (Go's float64 is IEEE binary64).\n2. **`expr`**: `Kind::Float(n) => crate::float::lit(*n)` — but wait, for the JS backend they emit `crate::float::lit(*n)` which is Rust's `n.to_string()`. For Go, emitting a float literal. Go's float literal syntax: `1.5`, `1e10`, etc. Rust's `f64::to_string()` produces e.g. `1.5`, `10000000000` for 1e10 (actually `10000000000`). Hmm, that would be an integer-looking literal in Go, which Go would type as an untyped constant that fits int... Actually in Go, `10000000000` as an untyped constant assigned to a float64 context would work, but as a literal in an arithmetic expression with float64 operands it would also work since untyped constants auto-convert. But there's a subtlety: Go constant arithmetic. If a Float literal is emitted as a plain decimal integer-looking token like `10000000000`, and it's used as e.g. `10000000000 / 2` where both are untyped constants... in Go, constant expressions are evaluated at arbitrary precision. `10000000000 / 2` is a constant expression = 5000000000, an untyped integer constant. If the expression context is float64, it converts fine. But consider `1e10` — Rust's `to_string()` for 1e10 gives `10000000000`. Hmm.\n\nWait, but there's a bigger subtlety with Go's constant folding, similar to why they made `tlInt`. In Go, a constant expression `1.5 + 2.5` is computed at compile time with arbitrary precision. For floats that's fine — binary64 values are exactly representable... no wait. `0.1 + 0.2` in Go constant arithmetic would be computed at arbitrary precision giving exactly 0.3, and then stored as float64 0.30000000000000004? Actually Go constant arithmetic computes in arbitrary precision and rounds only when converting to a float64 at the end. So `var x float64 = 0.1 + 0.2` gives 0.30000000000000004 (the same as at runtime). Actually the rounding happens once at assignment. So constant folding of float expressions is actually fine for IEEE semantics because the operations get rounded once at the end, whereas runtime IEEE semantics rounds after each op. These differ!\n\nExample: `0.1 + 0.2 - 0.3`. IEEE runtime: (0.1+0.2) = 0.30000000000000004, minus 0.3 = 5.55e-17. Go constant: 0.1+0.2-0.3 computed in arbitrary precision = 0.0 exactly. Then converted to float64 = 0.0. These differ!\n\nSo the same constant-folding escape needed for Int applies to Float: pass every float literal through a function so the expression is non-constant and evaluated at runtime with IEEE semantics. This is exactly the JS comment: \"The node's type picks which constant-folding escape a literal goes through... tlInt for an Int, tlInt64 for an Int64.\"\n\nSo I need a `tlFloat(n float64) float64 { return n }` helper, and `Kind::Float(n)` emits `tlFloat(...)`. But wait — what literal form? Go float64 literal: `tlFloat(1.5)`. Go's literal syntax allows `1.5`, `1e10`, `.5`, etc. Rust's `to_string()` output: for 1e10, `10000000000` — as a Go untyped constant, that's an integer constant, fine to pass to `tlFloat(float64)` param... actually `tlFloat(10000000000)` — the arg is untyped constant 10000000000, which fits int64? 1e10 = 10,000,000,000 > int32 max (2^31-1 ≈ 2.1e9) but fits int64. As an untyped constant it converts to float64 fine. Actually Go untyped integer constants convert to float64 fine when the context requires float64. `tlFloat(10000000000)` where tlFloat's param is float64 — Go will convert the untyped constant 10000000000 to float64. That works.\n\nBut what about very large ints, e.g. `1e308` → Rust to_string gives `` (309 digits). That's a valid Go untyped integer constant? Go untyped integer constants are arbitrary precision, so yes it's fine, converts to float64.\n\nHmm, but actually Rust's `to_string()` for 1e308... let me think. Rust's Display for f64: 1e308 → \"1e308\". Actually Rust prints `1e308` as `1e308`? Let me recall: Rust f64 Display uses shortest round-trip formatting that produces e.g. `1e308` for large values. For 1e10, Rust prints `10000000000`. For 1.5e308, prints `1.5e308`. So large floats use exponent notation which is valid Go syntax: `1.5e308`. And small ones like `10000000000` are integer-looking but valid as untyped constants.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: Rust prints integer-looking floats like `10000000000` — Go treats `10000000000` as an untyped integer constant, which is fine when it converts to float64. But what about `1e10`? Rust prints `10000000000` too? Hmm, actually let me verify. Rust's `f64::to_string()` uses the shortest representation that round-trips, and it may print `10000000000` or `1e10`. Let me check what JS `String(1e10)` gives: `10000000000`. Rust Display for `1e10_f64`: I believe it gives `10000000000` as well. Actually Rust's shortest representation tries decimal notation first and uses exponent only when it's shorter. For 1e10, decimal `10000000000` is 11 chars, exponent `1e10` is 4 chars, so Rust might print `1e10`. Hmm. Let me just test.\n\nActually the cleanest: I should match whatever the JS backend does (it emits `crate::float::lit(*n)` = Rust `n.to_string()`) to keep byte-for-byte agreement across backends. Go just needs to accept that token as a float literal. The safest is to check whether Rust's output is always valid Go float syntax.\n\nLet me test Rust's f64 to_string for various values. I'll write a quick Rust snippet... but writing to /tmp is denied for bash file writes, and I can't use heredocs. But I can write a file with the write tool? The denial says \"writes under /tmp\" — that's for bash redirections. Actually the constraint says \"CREATE AND EDIT FILES ONLY WITH YOUR write/edit TOOLS\". And \"writes under /tmp\" is listed as a known denial for bash. I could use the write tool to create a file in the worktree... but I shouldn't pollute the repo. Actually scratch files are fine (just don't git add them). But running `cargo run` for a scratch is denied (\"direct binary execution\"). Hmm, but I can add a corpus test case to check behavior via `just check`.\n\nActually, let me look at how the Python and Rust sibling implementations might do it — they're not done yet. Let me look at whether there's any guidance in plans/ or the ADR about Go float literal printing. Also, let me check what the corpus tests look like and whether there's a precedent for how a backend emits float literals (e.g., in emit_toylang.rs which uses crate::float::lit).\n\nActually, the simplest robust approach for Go: emit `tlFloat(...)` where the literal is Rust's `to_string()` output but ensure it's a valid Go float literal. Let me verify Rust's output is always a valid Go float literal:\n- `0.1` → \"0.1\" ✓\n- `1e10` → need to check\n- `1.5e308` → \"1.5e308\"? valid Go ✓\n- `5e-324` (smallest subnormal) → \"5e-324\" valid Go ✓\n\nThe concern is integer-looking output like \"10000000000\". In Go, `tlFloat(10000000000)` — arg is untyped integer constant, converts to float64. Valid. But wait, what about a huge integer-looking literal, e.g. 1e300 → Rust prints \"1e300\" (exponent form) since decimal would be 301 chars. Actually Rust uses exponent notation when it's shorter, so huge values always get exponent notation. And tiny values get exponent too. So the integer-looking case only happens for values like 1e10, 1e15 where decimal is shorter. Those are fine as Go integer constants converting to float64.\n\nBut actually there IS a subtle Go issue: an untyped integer constant that's passed to a float64 parameter gets converted. `tlFloat(10000000000)` → fine. But what if the float is used directly in a float expression without the tlFloat wrapper? We're wrapping in tlFloat always, so the arg is an untyped constant. But wait — even inside `tlFloat(10000000000)`, Go converts to float64 exactly? The untyped constant 10000000000 is exactly representable as float64, yes. And for a value like Rust's \"1.5e308\", Go parses it as a float literal exactly to the binary64 value. Good.\n\nHmm, but is there a case where Rust's to_string gives something NOT valid Go? E.g. NaN, Infinity — but those are non-finite and float::lit panics (they can't be literals; they're produced by arithmetic). Negative zero? Rust prints \"-0\". Go: `tlFloat(-0)` — is `-0` a valid literal? In Go, `-0` is the unary minus applied to the constant 0, an integer constant 0 (negative). As an untyped constant it's 0. Converting to float64 gives +0.0, not -0.0! That's a bug — but can a Float literal be -0? Negative zero arises from `-0.0`? Let me think: `-0.5` is a float literal via unary minus on 0.5. But can a literal -0 exist? In toylang, is `-0.0` a Float literal? Actually 0.0 is a float literal; -0.0 would be parsed as unary minus applied to 0.0 literal? Let me check parse/check. In check/mod.rs line 1852: `if let Expr::Float { value, .. } = base.as_ref() { return Ok(Tir::new(Type::Float, Kind::Float(-value))); }` — so a negated float literal produces Kind::Float with negated value. So -0.0 becomes Kind::Float(-0.0). Rust's `(-0.0f64).to_string()` = \"-0\". So `tlFloat(-0)` in Go gives +0, WRONG (should be -0.0).\n\nHmm wait, is that reachable? The negative literal folding: `Expr::Float { value, .. }` with base being a Float literal. -0.0 → value = -0.0 stored. float::lit(-0.0): `-0.0.is_finite()` is true, so it returns \"-0\". So Go would emit `tlFloat(-0)` → +0. That's a discrepancy.\n\nHow does JS handle this? JS emits `crate::float::lit(*n)` = \"-0\". In JS, `-0` is the literal -0.0 (negative zero), because JS `-0` is a number literal and negative zero is preserved! So JS gets -0.0 correct, but Go gets +0.0. Byte-for-byte agreement broken.\n\nHmm. But wait — would the corpus even test -0.0? It's an edge case. Let me think about how to handle it properly in Go. I could emit a form that preserves negative zero. In Go, `tlFloat(-0)` — the issue is `-0` is untyped integer 0. But if I emit it as a float literal, e.g. `-0.0` or better, use a token that Go parses as a float: `tlFloat(-0.0)`. Hmm, but actually Go's conversion of the untyped constant -0.0 (a float constant) to float64 gives negative zero? Let me think. In Go, the constant expression `-0.0` is an untyped floating-point constant with value negative zero. Converting an untyped constant -0.0 to float64 yields -0.0 (Go preserves negative zero in float constants? Actually Go spec says floating-point constants are rounded to the nearest representable; -0.0 is representable as float64 and Go does preserve it). Yes, in Go `var f float64 = -0.0; fmt.Println(f)` prints \"-0\" (negative zero). So `tlFloat(-0.0)` gives -0.0. Good.\n\nBut this is getting into the weeds. Does Rust's to_string for -0.0 produce \"-0\" or \"-0.0\"? It produces \"-0\". And for -1e10? \"-10000000000\" or \"-1e10\". Hmm.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. Maybe I should not overthink and just check what the JS commit's test/corpus covers and what the \"not yet settled: exact float literal printing\" issue says. The issue says: \"Not yet settled, needs a call during implementation: exact float literal printing so all seven backends agree byte for byte... pick a format and hold every backend to it.\" The reference JS commit used `crate::float::lit` (Rust's to_string) as the canonical literal spelling. So every backend emits the same literal string. For Go I should emit `crate::float::lit(*n)` too (well, wrapped in tlFloat to avoid constant folding).\n\nThe -0 case: Rust to_string gives \"-0\" which is not a Go float literal (it's an int constant expression). To make Go handle it, I need to ensure the emitted token is valid. `-0` as `tlFloat(-0)` → converts untyped int 0 → +0.0. That's a real discrepancy but an extreme edge case (a literal -0.0 in source). Would the corpus test it? Probably not, but correctness matters. \n\nLet me check: how does the Go backend currently deal with this kind of thing — is there a float formatting consideration documented? Let me search plans/questions.md Q37 and the ADR for what they decided about literal printing.\n\nActually, let me focus. Let me check:\n1. What does Rust's f64 to_string produce for the values in likely corpus tests?\n2. Whether `strconv.FormatFloat` or Go's `fmt.Sprintf(\"%g\")` matches Rust's shortest round-trip.\n\nActually the cleaner approach for the printer (show): JS uses `String(value)` for Float which is JS's shortest round-trip — matches Rust's to_string for the same double (both are shortest round-trip, Grisu/Ryū based). For Go, the equivalent is `strconv.FormatFloat(v, 'g', -1, 64)` which gives shortest round-trip. Let me verify: Go's `strconv.FormatFloat(f, 'g', -1, 64)` produces the shortest string that round-trips to the same float64. That matches JS String() and Rust to_string for normal values. \n\nBut there are differences in edge cases:\n- Go 'g' format: for 1e10, Go prints \"1e+10\"? No — `%g` with -1 precision: `strconv.FormatFloat(1e10, 'g', -1, 64)` = \"1e+10\". But JS String(1e10) = \"10000000000\". And Rust to_string(1e10) = \"10000000000\". Discrepancy! Go's 'g' format always uses exponent when the exponent is large (>= 21 or < -4, like C's %g), and formats exponent as \"e+10\" not \"e10\".\n\nHmm, that's a problem for byte-for-byte agreement in PRINTING a Float (e.g. a Float in a vector result). The corpus tests check output across backends. If a Float result prints as `1e+10` on Go but `10000000000` on JS/Rust, the harness fails.\n\nWait, but how does the JS backend print? `String(value)`. For a Float literal 1e10 printed... JS String(1e10) = \"10000000000\". Go strconv.FormatFloat(1e10,'g',-1,64) = \"1e+10\". These don't match!\n\nHmm, so what format should Go use? Let me think about what JS does for various values and find Go's equivalent.\n\nJS Number.prototype.toString() (which String() calls):\n- 1e21 and above: exponent form\n- Below 1e21: decimal notation\n- Small numbers < 1e-6: exponent form\n- Otherwise decimal\n\nGo's %g with -1 precision: uses exponent if exponent < -4 or >= 21 (for %g, it's exponent if exp < -4 || exp >= precision; with precision -1 → shortest, and the threshold is exp < -4 || exp > 21? Actually Go %g uses exponent if exp < -4 || exp >= 21? Let me recall C printf %g: exponent used if exponent < -4 or exponent >= precision (default 6). Go with -1 precision: it's the shortest representation, and the exponent threshold... Go's strconv 'g' with prec=-1 → shortest round trip, and it uses the same rule as %g: \"e\" format used if exponent < -4 or exponent >= 21? Hmm, I need to verify.\n\nLet me just test in Go directly. But I can't run Go binaries... wait, `go` might be available. Actually I can run `go run` — that's not cargo run of the toylang binary. The denial is about running the toylang binary directly, and multi-command bash lines. A single `go run` command should be fine. Actually, let me be careful — \"direct binary execution such as `cargo run -- run some_file.toy` or `./target/debug/toylang ...` (never runnable, no exceptions)\". `go run` is not the toylang binary, it's Go's toolchain. That should be allowed. But writing a Go scratch file... I'd need to write it with the write tool (can't use heredoc/redirection). I can write to /tmp? \"writes under /tmp\" is denied for bash. But the write tool can write anywhere presumably. Actually the denial list is about bash commands. Let me just use the write tool to create a scratch .go file, run `go run` on it, and never git add it.\n\nActually, simpler: there might be an existing test helper that runs Go. The test harness (tests/backend_go.rs) runs the go toolchain. Let me look at how tests run the Go backend and whether `go` is installed.\n\nLet me step back and look at the sibling implementations for guidance on the agreed format. Since the Python/Rust lanes are in progress but not landed, let me check if they exist as branches/commits I can reference. `git branch -a`. Actually the task says \"sibling in-progress float-build-rust/float-build-python lanes\" — they may have commits in their own worktrees not in this repo. But maybe there's a shared decision. Let me check plans/questions.md Q37.\n\nLet me look at the ADR and plans/questions for the float literal printing decision.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0635ff3d3001UEeICopa874YHt","sessionID":"ses_f9ca2427bffeVwNvwc6e496rx3","messageID":"msg_0635e564a001UrOwIYQJv52Kdx"}}
