{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788373628850,"sessionID":"ses_f9ca2427bffeVwNvwc6e496rx3","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a1746710381fb45e","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-go/docs/adr","pattern":"0007|binary64|shortest|round.trip|float"},"output":"Found 8 matches\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-go/docs/adr/0007-float-is-javascripts-double.md:\n  Line 5: # Float is JavaScript's number: an IEEE 754 binary64 double\n\n  Line 10: The float type is exactly the standard double every JavaScript engine carries -- IEEE 754\n\n  Line 11: binary64 -- with no alternative width and no decimal type. The supporting facts are already in\n\n  Line 15: setting it. Picking anything else would mean emulating a second float on the two targets that\n\n  Line 18: Not decided here, named so they are not assumed: how float literals print (six backends must\n\n  Line 19: agree byte for byte, and default float formatting differs across them), what `NaN` and\n\n  Line 22: associativity to make reassociation legitimate, and on keeping floating-point contraction off,\n\n  Line 24: [draft.md's Q37](../../plans/questions.md#q37-how-do-floats-print-and-what-are-nan-and-infinity-in-a-json-shaped-value-model).\n","metadata":{"matches":8,"truncated":false},"title":"0007|binary64|shortest|round.trip|float","time":{"start":1788373628827,"end":1788373628847}},"id":"prt_0635ff545001Bv8sV6tQxzKSUi","sessionID":"ses_f9ca2427bffeVwNvwc6e496rx3","messageID":"msg_0635e564a001UrOwIYQJv52Kdx"}}
