{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788373632781,"sessionID":"ses_f9ca2427bffeVwNvwc6e496rx3","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-81d4e2ddceb6c3a1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-go/docs/adr/0007-float-is-javascripts-double.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-go/docs/adr/0007-float-is-javascripts-double.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: status: accepted\n3: ---\n4: \n5: # Float is JavaScript's number: an IEEE 754 binary64 double\n6: \n7: Decided 2026-08-27, ahead of implementation: no `Float` exists in the checker yet, and `3.14`\n8: appears only in the overview page's [values list](../overview/vision.md#values).\n9: \n10: The float type is exactly the standard double every JavaScript engine carries -- IEEE 754\n11: binary64 -- with no alternative width and no decimal type. The supporting facts are already in\n12: the repository: it is the one numeric representation every backend has natively (for\n13: JavaScript and jq it is the *only* one), and the Int decision's carrying measurements\n14: established the double's 53-bit integer ceiling as the portable envelope, with JavaScript\n15: setting it. Picking anything else would mean emulating a second float on the two targets that\n16: have only this one, to gain a width nothing asked for.\n17: \n18: Not decided here, named so they are not assumed: how float literals print (six backends must\n19: agree byte for byte, and default float formatting differs across them), what `NaN` and\n20: `Infinity` mean in a language whose values are JSON-shaped (JSON has no spelling for either),\n21: and reduction semantics -- draft.md's vectorization sections already lean on `fold` declaring\n22: associativity to make reassociation legitimate, and on keeping floating-point contraction off,\n23: but those are operation questions, not representation ones. These are tracked as\n24: [draft.md's Q37](../../plans/questions.md#q37-how-do-floats-print-and-what-are-nan-and-infinity-in-a-json-shaped-value-model).\n\n(End of file - total 24 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\nstatus: accepted\n---\n\n# Float is JavaScript's number: an IEEE 754 binary64 double\n\nDecided 2026-08-27, ahead of implementation: no `Float` exists in the checker yet, and `3.14`\nappears only in the overview page's [values list](../overview/vision.md#values).\n\nThe float type is exactly the standard double every JavaScript engine carries -- IEEE 754\nbinary64 -- with no alternative width and no decimal type. The supporting facts are already in\nthe repository: it is the one numeric representation every backend has natively (for\nJavaScript and jq it is the *only* one), and the Int decision's carrying measurements\nestablished the double's 53-bit integer ceiling as the portable envelope, with JavaScript\nsetting it. Picking anything else would mean emulating a second float on the two targets that\nhave only this one, to gain a width nothing asked for.\n\nNot decided here, named so they are not assumed: how float literals print (six backends must\nagree byte for byte, and default float formatting differs across them), what `NaN` and\n`Infinity` mean in a language whose values are JSON-shaped (JSON has no spelling for either),","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-go/docs/adr/0007-float-is-javascripts-double.md","text":"---\nstatus: accepted\n---\n\n# Float is JavaScript's number: an IEEE 754 binary64 double\n\nDecided 2026-08-27, ahead of implementation: no `Float` exists in the checker yet, and `3.14`\nappears only in the overview page's [values list](../overview/vision.md#values).\n\nThe float type is exactly the standard double every JavaScript engine carries -- IEEE 754\nbinary64 -- with no alternative width and no decimal type. The supporting facts are already in\nthe repository: it is the one numeric representation every backend has natively (for\nJavaScript and jq it is the *only* one), and the Int decision's carrying measurements\nestablished the double's 53-bit integer ceiling as the portable envelope, with JavaScript\nsetting it. Picking anything else would mean emulating a second float on the two targets that\nhave only this one, to gain a width nothing asked for.\n\nNot decided here, named so they are not assumed: how float literals print (six backends must\nagree byte for byte, and default float formatting differs across them), what `NaN` and\n`Infinity` mean in a language whose values are JSON-shaped (JSON has no spelling for either),\nand reduction semantics -- draft.md's vectorization sections already lean on `fold` declaring\nassociativity to make reassociation legitimate, and on keeping floating-point contraction off,\nbut those are operation questions, not representation ones. These are tracked as\n[draft.md's Q37](../../plans/questions.md#q37-how-do-floats-print-and-what-are-nan-and-infinity-in-a-json-shaped-value-model).","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":24,"totalLines":24,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/adr/0007-float-is-javascripts-double.md","time":{"start":1788373632765,"end":1788373632779}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me read the ADR fully and the Q37 in plans/questions.md for the float literal printing decision.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_063600542001SylRRNAIJLUYSS","sessionID":"ses_f9ca2427bffeVwNvwc6e496rx3","messageID":"msg_0635ff7dd001BVmCOlsId1klbD"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788373633182,"sessionID":"ses_f9ca2427bffeVwNvwc6e496rx3","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-83ed61edbed786d4","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-go/plans/questions.md","pattern":"Q37"},"output":"Found 17 matches\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-go/plans/board-archive.yaml:\n  Line 983:   title: \"Grill Q37 -- ruled: admit NaN/Infinity, division by zero returns Infinity, matches IEEE (gh:145)\"\n\n\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-go/plans/questions.md:\n  Line 57: | [Q37](#q37-how-do-floats-print-and-what-are-nan-and-infinity-in-a-json-shaped-value-model) | How do floats print, and what are NaN and Infinity in a JSON-shaped value model? | RULED (gh:145): admit NaN/Infinity, division by zero returns Infinity (matches IEEE). Printing format is still open per-backend conformance work; tracked at board row `float-build` |\n\n  Line 538: ### Q37. How do floats print, and what are NaN and Infinity in a JSON-shaped value model?\n\n\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-go/plans/incidents/issue-149-20260902/20260901-232217-issue-149.jsonl.tail:\n  Line 3: {\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1788297746153,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fa1260973ffeOIaNGhvLCc80qv\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"bash\",\"callID\":\"chatcmpl-tool-b8a6d0c24bf29d23\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"gh issue view 149\"},\"output\":\"title:\\tImplement Float (IEEE binary64 per ADR 0007) across the backends\\nstate:\\tOPEN\\nauthor:\\tkantord (Dániel Kántor)\\nlabels:\\t\\ncomments:\\t0\\nassignees:\\t\\nprojects:\\t\\nmilestone:\\t\\nissue-type:\\t\\nparent:\\t\\nsub-issues:\\t\\nsub-issues-completed:\\t\\nblocked-by:\\t\\nblocking:\\t\\nnumber:\\t149\\n--\\nBuild the Float type end to end, per docs/adr/0007-float-is-javascripts-double.md: IEEE 754\\nbinary64, matching JavaScript's number representation, no alternative width, no decimal type.\\n\\nSemantics per gh:145 (the Q37 ruling): admit NaN/Infinity, division by zero returns Infinity\\n(IEEE), matching IEEE 754. Division is a total function on Float; Int and Float division have\\ndifferent failure behavior (Int still raises on zero divisor).\\n\\nNot yet settled, needs a call during implementation: exact float literal printing so all seven\\nbackends agree byte for byte (default float formatting differs across languages) -- pick a\\nformat and hold every backend to it. See draft.md Q37 in plans/questions.md for the fuller\\nquestion shape if it still exists, otherwise use the ADR and gh:145 as the source of truth.\\n\\nAcceptance:\\n- Float literals parse and type-check\\n- arithmetic (+ - * /) and comparisons work across all seven backends with agreeing output\\n- NaN/Infinity are producible and print identically across backends\\n- division by zero on Float returns Infinity, not an error (contrast with Int)\\n- corpus test coverage added for the above\\n\\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\\n\",\"metadata\":{\"output\":\"title:\\tImplement Float (IEEE binary64 per ADR 0007) across the backends\\nstate:\\tOPEN\\nauthor:\\tkantord (Dániel Kántor)\\nlabels:\\t\\ncomments:\\t0\\nassignees:\\t\\nprojects:\\t\\nmilestone:\\t\\nissue-type:\\t\\np...\n  Line 16: {\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1788297758639,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fa1260973ffeOIaNGhvLCc80qv\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"bash\",\"callID\":\"chatcmpl-tool-80dd1336ca70a8ea\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"cat docs/adr/0007-float-is-javascripts-double.md\"},\"output\":\"---\\nstatus: accepted\\n---\\n\\n# Float is JavaScript's number: an IEEE 754 binary64 double\\n\\nDecided 2026-08-27, ahead of implementation: no `Float` exists in the checker yet, and `3.14`\\nappears only in the overview page's [values list](../overview/vision.md#values).\\n\\nThe float type is exactly the standard double every JavaScript engine carries -- IEEE 754\\nbinary64 -- with no alternative width and no decimal type. The supporting facts are already in\\nthe repository: it is the one numeric representation every backend has natively (for\\nJavaScript and jq it is the *only* one), and the Int decision's carrying measurements\\nestablished the double's 53-bit integer ceiling as the portable envelope, with JavaScript\\nsetting it. Picking anything else would mean emulating a second float on the two targets that\\nhave only this one, to gain a width nothing asked for.\\n\\nNot decided here, named so they are not assumed: how float literals print (six backends must\\nagree byte for byte, and default float formatting differs across them), what `NaN` and\\n`Infinity` mean in a language whose values are JSON-shaped (JSON has no spelling for either),\\nand reduction semantics -- draft.md's vectorization sections already lean on `fold` declaring\\nassociativity to make reassociation legitimate, and on keeping floating-point contraction off,\\nbut those are operation questions, not representation ones. These are tracked as\\n[draft.md's Q37](../../plans/questions.md#q37-how-do-floats-print-and-what-are-nan-and-infinity-in-a-json-shaped-value-model).\\n\",\"metadata\":{\"output\":\"---\\nstatus: accepted\\n---\\n\\n# Float is JavaScript's number: an IEEE 754 binary64 double\\n\\nDecided 2026-08-27, ahead of implementation: no `F...\n  Line 25: {\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1788297772400,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fa1260973ffeOIaNGhvLCc80qv\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"bash\",\"callID\":\"chatcmpl-tool-9a4a50bc43bf1c07\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"grep -n \\\"Q37\\\" plans/questions.md 2>/dev/null | head; echo \\\"===\\\"; ls plans/\"},\"output\":\"57:| [Q37](#q37-how-do-floats-print-and-what-are-nan-and-infinity-in-a-json-shaped-value-model) | How do floats print, and what are NaN and Infinity in a JSON-shaped value model? | RULED (gh:145): admit NaN/Infinity, division by zero returns Infinity (matches IEEE). Printing format is still open per-backend conformance work; tracked at board row `float-build` |\\n532:### Q37. How do floats print, and what are NaN and Infinity in a JSON-shaped value model?\\n===\\nbenchmark-plan.md\\nbenchmark-suite-spike.md\\nbenchmark-tooling-spike.md\\nboard-archive.yaml\\nboard.yaml\\nchecker-structure.md\\ncrate-simplification.md\\ndocs-site.md\\ndraft-split.md\\nemuto-survey.md\\nenums.md\\neuler-ergonomics.md\\nlanguage-oddities.md\\nlanguage-precedents.md\\nmatcher-parser-spike.md\\nmini-parser-spike.md\\nopencode-rollout.md\\nopt-as-enum.md\\nprelude-inventory.md\\nprototype_1_5.md\\nprototype_1_5_step_1.md\\nprototype_1_5_step_2.md\\nprototype_1_5_step_3.md\\nprototype_1_5_step_4.md\\nprototype_1_5_step_5.md\\nprototype_1_5_step_6.md\\nprototype_1.md\\nprototype_1_step_1.md\\nprototype_1_step_2.md\\nprototype_1_step_3.md\\nprototype_1_step_4.md\\nprototype_1_step_5.md\\nquality-practices.md\\nquestions.md\\nstreams.md\\nstring-type-spike.md\\ntype-flow.md\\nworker-pool.md\\n\",\"metadata\":{\"output\":\"57:| [Q37](#q37-how-do-floats-print-and-what-are-nan-and-infinity-in-a-json-shaped-value-model) | How do floats print, and what are NaN and Infinity in a JSON-shaped value model? | RULED (gh:145): admit NaN/Infinity, division by zero returns Infinity (matches IEEE). Printing format is still open per-backend conformance work; tracked at board row `float-build` |\\n532:### Q37. How do floats print, and what are NaN and I...\n  Line 29: {\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1788297787926,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fa1260973ffeOIaNGhvLCc80qv\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"callID\":\"chatcmpl-tool-abd3942caa09f05b\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"filePath\":\"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-149/plans/questions.md\",\"offset\":525,\"limit\":70},\"output\":\"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-149/plans/questions.md</path>\\n<type>file</type>\\n<content>\\n525: calling a private helper.\\n526: \\n527: None of these were needed to get one function (`unlines`) out of six backends' worth of hand-\\n528: written codegen, and building them speculatively risks shaping them around a prelude that has\\n529: exactly one function in it. What would force an answer: a second prelude function needing an\\n530: internal helper, at which point the non-`pub`-is-simply-absent rule stops being free.\\n531: \\n532: ### Q37. How do floats print, and what are NaN and Infinity in a JSON-shaped value model?\\n533: \\n534: The representation is [decided](../draft.md#decided-float-is-javascripts-double): IEEE 754 binary64,\\n535: JavaScript's number. Everything observable about it is not, and each piece has to survive the\\n536: agreement harness, which checks bytes.\\n537: \\n538: - **Printing.** Every backend must render the same double to the same text, and their defaults\\n539:   do not agree on shortest-roundtrip versus fixed formatting, or on `1e21`-style switchover\\n540:   points. The printer is currently the only specification of output format\\n541:   ([Q35](#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return)), which makes\\n542:   this a per-backend conformance rule to be stated by hand, the same lesson as\\n543:   [backends can agree and still be wrong](../research-log/backends-can-agree-and-still-be-wrong.md).\\n544: - **NaN and Infinity.** IEEE produces both; JSON can spell neither. A language whose values\\n545:   are JSON-shaped either forbids them (a check on every producing operation), maps them to...\n\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-go/plans/incidents/issue-158-20260902/20260901-232216-issue-158.jsonl.tail:\n  Line 16: {\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1788297753521,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"callID\":\"chatcmpl-tool-986cb938641ab4a1\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"filePath\":\"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/plans/questions.md\",\"offset\":480,\"limit\":120},\"output\":\"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/plans/questions.md</path>\\n<type>file</type>\\n<content>\\n480: ### Q35. What are stdout and stderr, and does a program write or return?\\n481: \\n482: This document mentions `stdout` once and `stderr` never. For a language whose subject is\\n483: transforming data on a command line, that is not deferral, it is an oversight, and it is recorded\\n484: here rather than quietly fixed because the absence shaped things: every question about streams so\\n485: far has been about values coming *in*.\\n486: \\n487: What exists is one answer by default. A program is an expression, its value is rendered by the\\n488: type-driven printer, and that is the whole of output. It has served: line-oriented output needed\\n489: no side effect, because a `Str` containing newlines already is line-oriented output.\\n490: \\n491: What it does not answer:\\n492: \\n493: - **Does a program write, or return?** Returning is what makes a program an expression and what\\n494:   keeps `map` reorderable, since a write is an effect and an effect is an ordering constraint.\\n495:   Writing is what a long-running filter over a stream has to do, because holding the output until\\n496:   the input ends is the thing streaming exists to avoid. These are not obviously reconcilable.\\n497: - **Is stderr in the language or under it?** Every backend refuses in its own words today, and the\\n498:   agreement harness deliberately checks only *that* they refuse. Making the message part of the\\n499:   language means six backends must agree on it.\\n500: - **Does output have a type?** Input does, and it is checked. Output is whatever the body renders\\n501:  ...\n  Line 87: {\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1788297879629,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"callID\":\"call_c3ab975212c44a6f927e3a5f\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"filePath\":\"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/plans/board.yaml\",\"limit\":120,\"offset\":100},\"output\":\"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/plans/board.yaml</path>\\n<type>file</type>\\n<content>\\n100:   soft: [concat-retirement]\\n101:   status: delegated\\n102: \\n103: - id: euler-slow-fragments\\n104:   issue: gh:90\\n105:   title: Decide the slow-fragment tier -- Euler 10 and 14 solve correctly but cost minutes on the weak backends\\n106:   kind: decide\\n107:   needs: []\\n108:   prio: 3\\n109:   status: todo\\n110: \\n111: - id: matcher-totality-and-alt-design\\n112:   title: Grill matcher totality measurement, alt-composition (one arm, many patterns), and the parser-combinator stress test\\n113:   kind: decide\\n114:   needs: [auto-matchers-design]\\n115:   prio: 1\\n116:   status: todo\\n117: \\n118: - id: matcher-model-clarification\\n119:   issue: gh:122\\n120:   title: Grill the matching system's conceptual model -- base matcher kinds, how conditions enter, composition with type matchers (maintainer compose note)\\n121:   kind: decide\\n122:   needs: []\\n123:   soft: [matcher-totality-and-alt-design]\\n124:   prio: 2\\n125:   status: todo\\n126: \\n127: - id: variant-types-flip\\n128:   title: Variants become capitalized types with lowercase constructors, per the auto-matchers ratification -- subtype rules planned first, then the migration (corpus, examples, README, and docs/guides+tutorial+reference)\\n129:   kind: build\\n130:   needs: [matcher-totality-and-alt-design]\\n131:   prio: 2\\n132:   model: fable\\n133:   status: todo\\n134: \\n135: - id: shell-out-design\\n136:   title: Grill the syntax for calling external CLI programs -- bash-convenient or better\\n137:   kind: decide\\n138:   needs: []\\n139:   status: todo\\n140: \\n141: - id: stdin-syntax-des...\n\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-go/plans/incidents/issue-158-20260902/20260831-003754-issue-158.jsonl.tail:\n  Line 20: {\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1788129502058,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"callID\":\"chatcmpl-tool-89e4a1290d8e68ed\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"filePath\":\"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/plans/questions.md\",\"offset\":470,\"limit\":90},\"output\":\"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/plans/questions.md</path>\\n<type>file</type>\\n<content>\\n470: \\n471: Worth being explicit that cheapness is not an argument. What is recorded here is that the cost of\\n472: identity is lower than it looked, not that the language wants it.\\n473: \\n474: [The enum decision](../draft.md#decided-enums-nominal-and-json-native) has since answered every bullet for\\n475: enums specifically: they are identities (exhaustiveness requires it), `enum` is the declaration\\n476: form, and variant constructors land in the value namespace with bare-until-ambiguous\\n477: resolution. Records are deliberately not carried along; the alias-or-identity question stays\\n478: open for them, and this entry now tracks only that half.\\n479: \\n480: ### Q35. What are stdout and stderr, and does a program write or return?\\n481: \\n482: This document mentions `stdout` once and `stderr` never. For a language whose subject is\\n483: transforming data on a command line, that is not deferral, it is an oversight, and it is recorded\\n484: here rather than quietly fixed because the absence shaped things: every question about streams so\\n485: far has been about values coming *in*.\\n486: \\n487: What exists is one answer by default. A program is an expression, its value is rendered by the\\n488: type-driven printer, and that is the whole of output. It has served: line-oriented output needed\\n489: no side effect, because a `Str` containing newlines already is line-oriented output.\\n490: \\n491: What it does not answer:\\n492: \\n493: - **Does a program write, or return?** Returning is what makes a program an expression and what\\n494:   keeps ...\n  Line 31: {\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1788129519819,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"callID\":\"chatcmpl-tool-955890c210e7bbe9\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"filePath\":\"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/plans/board.yaml\"},\"output\":\"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/plans/board.yaml</path>\\n<type>file</type>\\n<content>\\n1: # The task board (scheduler v2, maintainer-specified 2026-08-29): every unblocked task is by\\n2: # definition ready to be picked up, and up to EIGHT build lanes run at once\\n3: # (raised from five 2026-08-30; decide rows queue for the maintainer separately\\n4: # and never consume a lane or a ready-set slot).\\n5: #\\n6: # Done rows do not live here (issue #113): landing a task MOVES its row to\\n7: # plans/board-archive.yaml (same schema, append-only) instead of flipping status in place, so\\n8: # this file stays the ~quarter of the board that is still live -- the loop's single most-read\\n9: # file, and every tick pays for its length. A needs/soft id not found on this live board is\\n10: # satisfied: it must have landed and been archived. Consult the archive only for provenance,\\n11: # never to decide whether something is blocking.\\n12: #\\n13: # Fields: id (slug; gh:N when a GitHub issue carries the spec), title, kind, needs, soft,\\n14: # prio, status.\\n15: #   kind: build  = delegatable implementation work (enwiro-delegate out, land-delegated-work in)\\n16: #         decide = needs a grilling/design session with the maintainer before anything builds\\n17: #   needs: HARD blockers -- forced order; a task with an un-done need is not pickable at all.\\n18: #          An id absent from this file is satisfied, not missing.\\n19: #   soft:  SOFT blockers -- convenient order that saves effort or time (file-footprint\\n20: #          overlaps, avoid-rework sequencing). A task with un-done soft blockers is still\\n21: #          pickable, just ranked below less-soft-blocked work...\n\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-go/plans/incidents/issue-149-20260902/20260830-220941-issue-149.jsonl.tail:\n  Line 4: {\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1788120586448,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fabb535ccffe1uMPqoZsBbW0jp\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"bash\",\"callID\":\"chatcmpl-tool-94d1c92f3c76906a\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"gh issue view 149\"},\"output\":\"title:\\tImplement Float (IEEE binary64 per ADR 0007) across the backends\\nstate:\\tOPEN\\nauthor:\\tkantord (Dániel Kántor)\\nlabels:\\t\\ncomments:\\t0\\nassignees:\\t\\nprojects:\\t\\nmilestone:\\t\\nissue-type:\\t\\nparent:\\t\\nsub-issues:\\t\\nsub-issues-completed:\\t\\nblocked-by:\\t\\nblocking:\\t\\nnumber:\\t149\\n--\\nBuild the Float type end to end, per docs/adr/0007-float-is-javascripts-double.md: IEEE 754\\nbinary64, matching JavaScript's number representation, no alternative width, no decimal type.\\n\\nSemantics per gh:145 (the Q37 ruling): admit NaN/Infinity, division by zero returns Infinity\\n(IEEE), matching IEEE 754. Division is a total function on Float; Int and Float division have\\ndifferent failure behavior (Int still raises on zero divisor).\\n\\nNot yet settled, needs a call during implementation: exact float literal printing so all seven\\nbackends agree byte for byte (default float formatting differs across languages) -- pick a\\nformat and hold every backend to it. See draft.md Q37 in plans/questions.md for the fuller\\nquestion shape if it still exists, otherwise use the ADR and gh:145 as the source of truth.\\n\\nAcceptance:\\n- Float literals parse and type-check\\n- arithmetic (+ - * /) and comparisons work across all seven backends with agreeing output\\n- NaN/Infinity are producible and print identically across backends\\n- division by zero on Float returns Infinity, not an error (contrast with Int)\\n- corpus test coverage added for the above\\n\\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\\n\",\"metadata\":{\"output\":\"title:\\tImplement Float (IEEE binary64 per ADR 0007) across the backends\\nstate:\\tOPEN\\nauthor:\\tkantord (Dániel Kántor)\\nlabels:\\t\\ncomments:\\t0\\nassignees:\\t\\nprojects:\\t\\nmilestone:\\t\\nissue-type:\\t\\np...\n  Line 7: {\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1788120589150,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fabb535ccffe1uMPqoZsBbW0jp\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"callID\":\"chatcmpl-tool-b7e22f327df21ec7\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"filePath\":\"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-149/docs/adr/0007-float-is-javascripts-double.md\"},\"output\":\"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-149/docs/adr/0007-float-is-javascripts-double.md</path>\\n<type>file</type>\\n<content>\\n1: ---\\n2: status: accepted\\n3: ---\\n4: \\n5: # Float is JavaScript's number: an IEEE 754 binary64 double\\n6: \\n7: Decided 2026-08-27, ahead of implementation: no `Float` exists in the checker yet, and `3.14`\\n8: appears only in the overview page's [values list](../overview/vision.md#values).\\n9: \\n10: The float type is exactly the standard double every JavaScript engine carries -- IEEE 754\\n11: binary64 -- with no alternative width and no decimal type. The supporting facts are already in\\n12: the repository: it is the one numeric representation every backend has natively (for\\n13: JavaScript and jq it is the *only* one), and the Int decision's carrying measurements\\n14: established the double's 53-bit integer ceiling as the portable envelope, with JavaScript\\n15: setting it. Picking anything else would mean emulating a second float on the two targets that\\n16: have only this one, to gain a width nothing asked for.\\n17: \\n18: Not decided here, named so they are not assumed: how float literals print (six backends must\\n19: agree byte for byte, and default float formatting differs across them), what `NaN` and\\n20: `Infinity` mean in a language whose values are JSON-shaped (JSON has no spelling for either),\\n21: and reduction semantics -- draft.md's vectorization sections already lean on `fold` declaring\\n22: associativity to make reassociation legitimate, and on keeping floating-point contraction off,\\n23: but those are operation questions, not representation ones. These are tracked as\\n24: [draft.md's Q37](../...\n  Line 9: {\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1788120589485,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fabb535ccffe1uMPqoZsBbW0jp\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"bash\",\"callID\":\"chatcmpl-tool-9e443a6e683abe96\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"gh issue view 145\"},\"output\":\"title:\\tRuling: Q37 float semantics -- admit NaN/Infinity, division by zero returns Infinity (IEEE)\\nstate:\\tOPEN\\nauthor:\\tkantord (Dániel Kántor)\\nlabels:\\t\\ncomments:\\t0\\nassignees:\\t\\nprojects:\\t\\nmilestone:\\t\\nissue-type:\\t\\nparent:\\t\\nsub-issues:\\t\\nsub-issues-completed:\\t\\nblocked-by:\\t\\nblocking:\\t\\nnumber:\\t145\\n--\\nGrilled via float-and-benchmarks.round.yaml, question q37-float-semantics.\\n\\n**Ruled: admit NaN/Infinity, division by zero returns Infinity, matching IEEE 754** -- overrides the recommended option (forbid NaN/Infinity, raise on zero divisor to match Int). Division stays a total function on Float; Int and Float division now have different failure behavior. Printing format for NaN/Infinity/shortest-roundtrip is still open per-backend conformance work.\\n\\nNo additional maintainer notes on this question.\\n\\nUnblocks board row float-build (needs: q37-float-semantics).\\n\",\"metadata\":{\"output\":\"title:\\tRuling: Q37 float semantics -- admit NaN/Infinity, division by zero returns Infinity (IEEE)\\nstate:\\tOPEN\\nauthor:\\tkantord (Dániel Kántor)\\nlabels:\\t\\ncomments:\\t0\\nassignees:\\t\\nprojects:\\t\\nmilestone:\\t\\nissue-type:\\t\\nparent:\\t\\nsub-issues:\\t\\nsub-issues-completed:\\t\\nblocked-by:\\t\\nblocking:\\t\\nnumber:\\t145\\n--\\nGrilled via float-and-benchmarks.round.yaml, question q37-float-semantics.\\n\\n**Ruled: admit NaN/Infinity, division by zero returns Infinity, matching IEEE 754** -- overrides the recommended option (forbid NaN/Infinity, raise on zero divisor to match Int). Division stays a total function on Float; Int and Float division now have different failure behavior. Printing format for NaN/Infinity/shortest-roundtrip is still open per-backend conformance work.\\n\\nNo additional maintainer notes on this quest...\n  Line 12: {\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1788120619505,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fabb535ccffe1uMPqoZsBbW0jp\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"bash\",\"callID\":\"chatcmpl-tool-b46d5eaccd14a213\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"ls -R src | head -80\"},\"output\":\"src:\\nast.rs\\ncheck\\nemit_go.rs\\nemit_jq.rs\\nemit_js.rs\\nemit_llvm.rs\\nemit_lua.rs\\nemit_py.rs\\nemit_rs.rs\\nemit_toylang.rs\\nerror.rs\\nfmt_tree.rs\\ninput.rs\\nlib.rs\\nmain.rs\\nparse.rs\\nprelude.rs\\ntags.rs\\ntir.rs\\nty.rs\\n\\nsrc/check:\\nlinearity.rs\\nmod.rs\\ntypes.rs\\n\",\"metadata\":{\"output\":\"src:\\nast.rs\\ncheck\\nemit_go.rs\\nemit_jq.rs\\nemit_js.rs\\nemit_llvm.rs\\nemit_lua.rs\\nemit_py.rs\\nemit_rs.rs\\nemit_toylang.rs\\nerror.rs\\nfmt_tree.rs\\ninput.rs\\nlib.rs\\nmain.rs\\nparse.rs\\nprelude.rs\\ntags.rs\\ntir.rs\\nty.rs\\n\\nsrc/check:\\nlinearity.rs\\nmod.rs\\ntypes.rs\\n\",\"exit\":0,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"ls -R src | head -80\",\"time\":{\"start\":1788120619500,\"end\":1788120619504}},\"metadata\":{\"openrouter\":{\"reasoning_details\":[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"Let me explore the codebase structure. Let me look at src/, tests/, and understand how types are implemented. Let me check the plans/questions.md Q37 too.\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]}},\"id\":\"prt_0544b4dde001AizkQIpa6CzXEJ\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_fabb535ccffe1uMPqoZsBbW0jp\",\"messageID\":\"msg_0544ae4c5001Y8poLsolOsKYkm\"}}\n\n  Line 14: {\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1788120632517,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fabb535ccffe1uMPqoZsBbW0jp\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"grep\",\"callID\":\"chatcmpl-tool-844f9c8abb7d7e4b\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"Q37\",\"path\":\"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-149/plans/questions.md\"},\"output\":\"Found 3 matches\\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-149/plans/board-archive.yaml:\\n  Line 983:   title: \\\"Grill Q37 -- ruled: admit NaN/Infinity, division by zero returns Infinity, matches IEEE (gh:145)\\\"\\n\\n\\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-149/plans/questions.md:\\n  Line 57: | [Q37](#q37-how-do-floats-print-and-what-are-nan-and-infinity-in-a-json-shaped-value-model) | How do floats print, and what are NaN and Infinity in a JSON-shaped value model? | RULED (gh:145): admit NaN/Infinity, division by zero returns Infinity (matches IEEE). Printing format is still open per-backend conformance work; tracked at board row `float-build` |\\n\\n  Line 532: ### Q37. How do floats print, and what are NaN and Infinity in a JSON-shaped value model?\\n\",\"metadata\":{\"matches\":3,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"Q37\",\"time\":{\"start\":1788120632495,\"end\":1788120632513}},\"id\":\"prt_0544b6db6001cnNKacubt3Ik5N\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_fabb535ccffe1uMPqoZsBbW0jp\",\"messageID\":\"msg_0544ae4c5001Y8poLsolOsKYkm\"}}\n\n  Line 17: {\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1788120633948,\"sessionID\":\"ses_fabb535ccffe1uMPqoZsBbW0jp\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"bash\",\"callID\":\"chatcmpl-tool-b99e2e586cd897f1\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"sed -n 520,620p plans/questions.md\"},\"output\":\"One file exists: `prelude.toy`, always merged in whole, `pub` picking which of its definitions a\\nprogram receives. What it does not have: a way to name what a program wants rather than receiving\\nall of it; a way for a program's own file to export something another file imports; more than one\\nfile to import from at all; and enforced privacy, since a non-`pub` prelude definition today is\\nnot \\\"private to the prelude,\\\" it is simply never compiled, which forecloses a `pub` function\\ncalling a private helper.\\n\\nNone of these were needed to get one function (`unlines`) out of six backends' worth of hand-\\nwritten codegen, and building them speculatively risks shaping them around a prelude that has\\nexactly one function in it. What would force an answer: a second prelude function needing an\\ninternal helper, at which point the non-`pub`-is-simply-absent rule stops being free.\\n\\n### Q37. How do floats print, and what are NaN and Infinity in a JSON-shaped value model?\\n\\nThe representation is [decided](../draft.md#decided-float-is-javascripts-double): IEEE 754 binary64,\\nJavaScript's number. Everything observable about it is not, and each piece has to survive the\\nagreement harness, which checks bytes.\\n\\n- **Printing.** Every backend must render the same double to the same text, and their defaults\\n  do not agree on shortest-roundtrip versus fixed formatting, or on `1e21`-style switchover\\n  points. The printer is currently the only specification of output format\\n  ([Q35](#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return)), which makes\\n  this a per-backend conformance rule to be stated by hand, the same lesson as\\n  [backends can agree and still be wrong](../research-log/backends-can-agre...","metadata":{"matches":17,"truncated":false},"title":"Q37","time":{"start":1788373633151,"end":1788373633177}},"id":"prt_063600702001HdrudDDQnpjvjI","sessionID":"ses_f9ca2427bffeVwNvwc6e496rx3","messageID":"msg_0635ff7dd001BVmCOlsId1klbD"}}
