{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788373635202,"sessionID":"ses_f9ca2427bffeVwNvwc6e496rx3","part":{"id":"prt_06360107a001cpDkqfvssdSALr","messageID":"msg_0636008b6001PeN3rgjE8Rme1N","sessionID":"ses_f9ca2427bffeVwNvwc6e496rx3","snapshot":"1decf50ab48e447df1a464a587f406917061a83f","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788373638164,"sessionID":"ses_f9ca2427bffeVwNvwc6e496rx3","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b0c808992678b574","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-go/plans/questions.md","offset":532,"limit":80},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-go/plans/questions.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n532: \n533: None of these were needed to get one function (`unlines`) out of six backends' worth of hand-\n534: written codegen, and building them speculatively risks shaping them around a prelude that has\n535: exactly one function in it. What would force an answer: a second prelude function needing an\n536: internal helper, at which point the non-`pub`-is-simply-absent rule stops being free.\n537: \n538: ### Q37. How do floats print, and what are NaN and Infinity in a JSON-shaped value model?\n539: \n540: The representation is [decided](../draft.md#decided-float-is-javascripts-double): IEEE 754 binary64,\n541: JavaScript's number. Everything observable about it is not, and each piece has to survive the\n542: agreement harness, which checks bytes.\n543: \n544: - **Printing.** Every backend must render the same double to the same text, and their defaults\n545:   do not agree on shortest-roundtrip versus fixed formatting, or on `1e21`-style switchover\n546:   points. The printer is currently the only specification of output format\n547:   ([Q35](#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return)), which makes\n548:   this a per-backend conformance rule to be stated by hand, the same lesson as\n549:   [backends can agree and still be wrong](../research-log/backends-can-agree-and-still-be-wrong.md).\n550: - **NaN and Infinity.** IEEE produces both; JSON can spell neither. A language whose values\n551:   are JSON-shaped either forbids them (a check on every producing operation), maps them to\n552:   something at the boundary (jq-style lossiness, the kind this design usually refuses), or\n553:   admits values its own output cannot carry.\n554: - **Division by zero.** The Int rule says a zero divisor is the only way arithmetic fails.\n555:   IEEE says `1.0 / 0.0` is `Infinity`, no failure at all. Keeping both means division's\n556:   behavior depends on its operand type; unifying means overriding one standard or the other.\n557: \n558: None of this blocks anything else, so it waits for `Float` to be forced by a real program the\n559: way `inputs` and `jsonlines` were.\n\n(End of file - total 559 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"\nNone of these were needed to get one function (`unlines`) out of six backends' worth of hand-\nwritten codegen, and building them speculatively risks shaping them around a prelude that has\nexactly one function in it. What would force an answer: a second prelude function needing an\ninternal helper, at which point the non-`pub`-is-simply-absent rule stops being free.\n\n### Q37. How do floats print, and what are NaN and Infinity in a JSON-shaped value model?\n\nThe representation is [decided](../draft.md#decided-float-is-javascripts-double): IEEE 754 binary64,\nJavaScript's number. Everything observable about it is not, and each piece has to survive the\nagreement harness, which checks bytes.\n\n- **Printing.** Every backend must render the same double to the same text, and their defaults\n  do not agree on shortest-roundtrip versus fixed formatting, or on `1e21`-style switchover\n  points. The printer is currently the only specification of output format\n  ([Q35](#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return)), which makes\n  this a per-backend conformance rule to be stated by hand, the same lesson as\n  [backends can agree and still be wrong](../research-log/backends-can-agree-and-still-be-wrong.md).\n- **NaN and Infinity.** IEEE produces both; JSON can spell neither. A language whose values\n  are JSON-shaped either forbids them (a check on every producing operation), maps them to","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-go/plans/questions.md","text":"\nNone of these were needed to get one function (`unlines`) out of six backends' worth of hand-\nwritten codegen, and building them speculatively risks shaping them around a prelude that has\nexactly one function in it. What would force an answer: a second prelude function needing an\ninternal helper, at which point the non-`pub`-is-simply-absent rule stops being free.\n\n### Q37. How do floats print, and what are NaN and Infinity in a JSON-shaped value model?\n\nThe representation is [decided](../draft.md#decided-float-is-javascripts-double): IEEE 754 binary64,\nJavaScript's number. Everything observable about it is not, and each piece has to survive the\nagreement harness, which checks bytes.\n\n- **Printing.** Every backend must render the same double to the same text, and their defaults\n  do not agree on shortest-roundtrip versus fixed formatting, or on `1e21`-style switchover\n  points. The printer is currently the only specification of output format\n  ([Q35](#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return)), which makes\n  this a per-backend conformance rule to be stated by hand, the same lesson as\n  [backends can agree and still be wrong](../research-log/backends-can-agree-and-still-be-wrong.md).\n- **NaN and Infinity.** IEEE produces both; JSON can spell neither. A language whose values\n  are JSON-shaped either forbids them (a check on every producing operation), maps them to\n  something at the boundary (jq-style lossiness, the kind this design usually refuses), or\n  admits values its own output cannot carry.\n- **Division by zero.** The Int rule says a zero divisor is the only way arithmetic fails.\n  IEEE says `1.0 / 0.0` is `Infinity`, no failure at all. Keeping both means division's\n  behavior depends on its operand type; unifying means overriding one standard or the other.\n\nNone of this blocks anything else, so it waits for `Float` to be forced by a real program the\nway `inputs` and `jsonlines` were.","lineStart":532,"lineEnd":559,"totalLines":559,"truncated":false}},"title":"plans/questions.md","time":{"start":1788373638157,"end":1788373638163}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"There's a lot of incident logs from the previous run that read Q37 but never committed. Let me read Q37 fully in questions.md to understand the printing decision, then look at how the JS backend prints floats (String()), and figure out how to make Go agree byte for byte.\n\nLet me read the full Q37 section in questions.md (offset 532 onward).","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_063601a18001MRaiT3zFlljoki","sessionID":"ses_f9ca2427bffeVwNvwc6e496rx3","messageID":"msg_0636008b6001PeN3rgjE8Rme1N"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788373638240,"sessionID":"ses_f9ca2427bffeVwNvwc6e496rx3","part":{"id":"prt_063601c5d001QL1J131uDpc78B","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"1decf50ab48e447df1a464a587f406917061a83f","messageID":"msg_0636008b6001PeN3rgjE8Rme1N","sessionID":"ses_f9ca2427bffeVwNvwc6e496rx3","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":67110,"input":8823,"output":101,"reasoning":74,"cache":{"write":0,"read":58112}},"cost":0.001534787}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788373639665,"sessionID":"ses_f9ca2427bffeVwNvwc6e496rx3","part":{"id":"prt_0636021e9001obnoe5Wxm4L840","messageID":"msg_063601c680017T6r1G3MAiTYGw","sessionID":"ses_f9ca2427bffeVwNvwc6e496rx3","snapshot":"1decf50ab48e447df1a464a587f406917061a83f","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788373672966,"sessionID":"ses_f9ca2427bffeVwNvwc6e496rx3","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8fd6c6a86ccb4bcc","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-go/tests/backend_go.rs"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-go/tests/backend_go.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: //! The Go backend against the things it alone can say.\n2: //!\n3: //! Behaviour lives in the corpus like every other backend. What is here is what falls out of Go\n4: //! being the first target that is statically typed with no runtime type information: it needs a\n5: //! declared name for every record, and it rejects an import nothing uses.\n6: \n7: /// A record type here is structural, so every spelling of `{name: Str, age: Int}` is one type\n8: /// however often it is written. Go is nominal and needs a declaration, so this is the first\n9: /// backend that has to decide how many types the program actually has -- and getting it wrong\n10: /// would mean two Go structs that no assignment between them would typecheck.\n11: #[test]\n12: fn one_struct_per_record_type() {\n13:     let src = r#\"\n14: fn keep(db: {users: Vec<{name: Str, age: Int}>}) -> Vec<{name: Str, age: Int}> = db.users\n15: fn name(u: {name: Str, age: Int}) -> Str = u.name\n16: \n17: keep(input)\n18: \"#;\n19:     let p = toylang::compile(src).unwrap();\n20:     let go = toylang::emit_go::emit(&p);\n21:     // The user record and the wrapper around it, and nothing more: the three occurrences of\n22:     // the user record are one type.\n23:     assert_eq!(\n24:         go.matches(\"type tlRec\").count(),\n25:         2,\n26:         \"one struct per record type:\\n{go}\"\n27:     );\n28: }\n29: \n30: /// An unused import does not compile in Go, so the import list cannot be padded the way an\n31: /// unused helper can. This is why the imports come from walking the program while the helpers\n32: /// are read back off the emitted text.\n33: #[test]\n34: fn imports_are_exactly_what_is_used() {\n35:     let strings_only = toylang::emit_go::emit(&toylang::compile(r#\"\"a\" + \"b\"\"#).unwrap());\n36:     assert!(\n37:         !strings_only.contains(\"strconv\"),\n38:         \"nothing here formats a number:\\n{strings_only}\"\n39:     );\n40:     assert!(\n41:         !strings_only.contains(\"encoding/json\"),\n42:         \"nothing here reads input:\\n{strings_only}\"\n43:     );\n44: \n45:     let with_int = toylang::emit_go::emit(&toylang::compile(\"[1, 2]\").unwrap());\n46:     assert!(\n47:         with_int.contains(\"\\\"strconv\\\"\"),\n48:         \"printing an Int needs strconv:\\n{with_int}\"\n49:     );\n50: }\n\n(End of file - total 50 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"//! The Go backend against the things it alone can say.\n//!\n//! Behaviour lives in the corpus like every other backend. What is here is what falls out of Go\n//! being the first target that is statically typed with no runtime type information: it needs a\n//! declared name for every record, and it rejects an import nothing uses.\n\n/// A record type here is structural, so every spelling of `{name: Str, age: Int}` is one type\n/// however often it is written. Go is nominal and needs a declaration, so this is the first\n/// backend that has to decide how many types the program actually has -- and getting it wrong\n/// would mean two Go structs that no assignment between them would typecheck.\n#[test]\nfn one_struct_per_record_type() {\n    let src = r#\"\nfn keep(db: {users: Vec<{name: Str, age: Int}>}) -> Vec<{name: Str, age: Int}> = db.users\nfn name(u: {name: Str, age: Int}) -> Str = u.name\n\nkeep(input)\n\"#;\n    let p = toylang::compile(src).unwrap();\n    let go = toylang::emit_go::emit(&p);","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-go/tests/backend_go.rs","text":"//! The Go backend against the things it alone can say.\n//!\n//! Behaviour lives in the corpus like every other backend. What is here is what falls out of Go\n//! being the first target that is statically typed with no runtime type information: it needs a\n//! declared name for every record, and it rejects an import nothing uses.\n\n/// A record type here is structural, so every spelling of `{name: Str, age: Int}` is one type\n/// however often it is written. Go is nominal and needs a declaration, so this is the first\n/// backend that has to decide how many types the program actually has -- and getting it wrong\n/// would mean two Go structs that no assignment between them would typecheck.\n#[test]\nfn one_struct_per_record_type() {\n    let src = r#\"\nfn keep(db: {users: Vec<{name: Str, age: Int}>}) -> Vec<{name: Str, age: Int}> = db.users\nfn name(u: {name: Str, age: Int}) -> Str = u.name\n\nkeep(input)\n\"#;\n    let p = toylang::compile(src).unwrap();\n    let go = toylang::emit_go::emit(&p);\n    // The user record and the wrapper around it, and nothing more: the three occurrences of\n    // the user record are one type.\n    assert_eq!(\n        go.matches(\"type tlRec\").count(),\n        2,\n        \"one struct per record type:\\n{go}\"\n    );\n}\n\n/// An unused import does not compile in Go, so the import list cannot be padded the way an\n/// unused helper can. This is why the imports come from walking the program while the helpers\n/// are read back off the emitted text.\n#[test]\nfn imports_are_exactly_what_is_used() {\n    let strings_only = toylang::emit_go::emit(&toylang::compile(r#\"\"a\" + \"b\"\"#).unwrap());\n    assert!(\n        !strings_only.contains(\"strconv\"),\n        \"nothing here formats a number:\\n{strings_only}\"\n    );\n    assert!(\n        !strings_only.contains(\"encoding/json\"),\n        \"nothing here reads input:\\n{strings_only}\"\n    );\n\n    let with_int = toylang::emit_go::emit(&toylang::compile(\"[1, 2]\").unwrap());\n    assert!(\n        with_int.contains(\"\\\"strconv\\\"\"),\n        \"printing an Int needs strconv:\\n{with_int}\"\n    );\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":50,"totalLines":50,"truncated":false}},"title":"tests/backend_go.rs","time":{"start":1788373672948,"end":1788373672964}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now let me understand the JS backend's printing behavior precisely. The JS commit uses:\n- `Kind::Float(n) => crate::float::lit(*n)` — literal spelling via Rust's `to_string()`\n- Printer: `String(value)` for Float.\n\nFor the JS printer, `String(value)` = `Number.prototype.toString()`:\n- shortest round-trip\n- values >= 1e21 or < 1e-6 use exponent notation like `1e+21`, `1e-7`\n- otherwise decimal\n\nRust's `to_string()` (Display for f64) produces the shortest round-trip but with different rules for exponent switchover? Actually Rust's Display uses a different algorithm. Let me check specific differences:\n- Rust: `1e10_f64.to_string()` = \"10000000000\"\n- JS: `String(1e10)` = \"10000000000\"  ✓ match\n- Rust: `1e21_f64.to_string()` = \"1e21\"? \n- JS: `String(1e21)` = \"1e+21\"\n\nHmm, Rust's Display for 1e21. Rust uses `to_shortest` algorithm that produces decimal notation when the exponent is in a certain range, exponent otherwise. Let me recall actual Rust output. I believe Rust Display for f64:\n- `1e20_f64.to_string()` = \"100000000000000000000\" (decimal)\n- `1e21_f64.to_string()` = \"1e21\" (exponent)\n\nActually I'm not 100% sure of the threshold. Rust's Display produces e.g. \"1e21\"? Let me just test it. I can write a small Rust test... but compiling Rust takes time. Actually I can check via existing code — the reference commit's `src/float.rs` uses `n.to_string()`. And JS emits `String(value)` for printing. For corpus agreement, JS program output for a Float is via String(), NOT via float::lit. float::lit is only for literal spelling in source (emitted as `1.5` in JS source code).\n\nWait — so there are two different formats:\n1. Literal spelling (float::lit) — used to emit a literal into backend source. In JS, `1.5` written as `1.5`.\n2. Printed output — JS uses String(value), which for a computed result like `1.5 + 0.25*2.0` = 2.0 prints \"2\".\n\nNote the JS test: `float_literals_parse_and_print` expects output \"2\" — because 1.5 + 0.5 = 2.0 prints as \"2\" (String(2.0) = \"2\").\n\nSo the printer format is the JS String() format = shortest round-trip with JS's switchover rules. Every backend must reproduce this EXACTLY. That's the conformance rule to state by hand.\n\nSo for Go, the printer must produce the same bytes as JS String() for every double. And the literal spelling must be valid Go.\n\nNow, what is JS String() format exactly? ECMA-262 Number.prototype.toString: produces the shortest string s such that converting s back gives the same Number, with:\n- If exponent e satisfies -6 < e <= 20 (i.e., e in [-5, 20]), use decimal notation (Integer or Fraction representation).\n- Otherwise use scientific notation \"1.5e+21\" style: digit \".\" digits \"e\" sign digits.\n\nSpecifically:\n- 1e-6 → \"0.000001\" (decimal)\n- 1e-7 → \"1e-7\" (scientific)\n- 1e21 → \"1e+21\" (scientific)\n- 1e20 → \"100000000000000000000\" (decimal)\n\nGo's strconv with 'g' and -1 precision: uses exponent notation when exponent < -4 || exponent >= 21 (approximately). Go 'g' -1: shortest round trip, and it prints with %e when the exponent is < -4 or >= 21? Let me check. Actually Go's `strconv.FormatFloat(f, 'g', -1, 64)`:\n- 1e-6 → \"1e-06\"? or \"0.000001\"? \n- 1e21 → \"1e+21\"\n- 1e20 → \"1e+20\" or decimal?\n\nI need to test this. Go's %g uses the same switchover as C printf: exponent used if exp < -4 || exp >= precision. With precision -1 (shortest), the \"precision\" is effectively the number of digits needed, so the switchover differs.\n\nLet me just test Go directly. I can write a scratch .go file with the write tool and run `go run`. But is `go` installed? The test harness needs Go. Let me check tests/backend_go.rs to see how it invokes Go and whether go is available.\n\nActually — the corpus agreement harness runs every backend. For the float-build-go lane, the acceptance is corpus test coverage \"added for the above\". But wait — if I add Float corpus cases, EVERY backend must agree on the output (output means they all print it and agree; refuses means they all decline). Since Float is only implemented in JS and Go (rust/python lanes in progress), adding Float corpus cases now would break the other 5 backends (they unreachable!/refuse). \n\nHmm. So how do corpus cases for a backend-specific feature work? Per AGENTS.md: \"A program that does not compile is not a corpus case.\" And \"Every case runs on every backend. output means they all print it and agree\". So Float corpus cases can't be added until all backends carry Float. That's why the JS commit put Float tests in tests/backend_js.rs (JS-only), not in the corpus!\n\nSo the JS reference put the tests in `tests/backend_js.rs` as backend-specific unit tests. For Go, I should mirror that: add Go Float tests in `tests/backend_go.rs`. The task brief says \"tests/backend_go.rs / tests/corpus.rs as needed\". Since corpus is cross-backend and Float isn't universal yet, backend_go.rs is the right place. Let me check what the rust/python sibling lanes did — they might have a pattern. But they're not landed. Let me check the incident logs or any hints.\n\nActually wait — maybe I should reconsider. The issue acceptance says \"corpus test coverage added for the above\". But AGENTS.md and the JS reference both put JS-only tests in backend_js.rs precisely because corpus requires all-backend agreement. The board title says \"One backend, one commit. following float-build's JS implementation as the reference.\" So following the JS reference: put Go Float tests in tests/backend_go.rs. When all backends land, a later row can promote them to corpus.\n\nLet me look at tests/backend_go.rs to see the current structure and how the harness runs Go.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_06360a27a001jXTHC653hued6t","sessionID":"ses_f9ca2427bffeVwNvwc6e496rx3","messageID":"msg_063601c680017T6r1G3MAiTYGw"}}
